OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026", "title": "A power saving for planar unit distances", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "167", "family_title": "Planar distinct distances and unit-distance bounds", "abstract_html": "We prove a power saving for the planar unit-distance problem: for some absolute \\(\\beta\\lt 4/3\\), every set of <i>n</i> points in the Euclidean plane determines \\(O(n^\\beta)\\) unordered pairs at unit distance.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a finite set \\(X\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\), define \\( u(X)=\\#\\bigl\\{\\{x,y\\}\\subset X:\\|x-y\\|_2=1\\bigr\\}, \\qquad u(n)=\\max_{\\substack{X\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\\\ |X|=n}}u(X). \\) The pairs are unordered and their endpoints are …", "statement": "<p>For a finite set \\(X\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\), define\n\\[\nu(X)=\\#\\bigl\\{\\{x,y\\}\\subset X:\\|x-y\\|_2=1\\bigr\\},\n\\qquad\nu(n)=\\max_{\\substack{X\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\\\ |X|=n}}u(X).\n\\]\nThe pairs are unordered and their endpoints are distinct.</p><p>There are absolute constants \\(0&lt;C&lt;\\infty\\) and \\(1\\leq\\beta&lt;4/3\\) such that\n\\[\nu(n)\\leq Cn^\\beta\n\\]\nfor every nonnegative integer \\(n\\).</p><p>Throughout the proof outline, asymptotic notation refers to a chosen sequence \\(t\\to\\infty\\). Constants denoted by \\(c,C\\) may change between occurrences. All logarithms are natural.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose no fixed power saving exists. For every \\(j\\geq2\\), failure of a bound with exponent \\(4/3-1/j\\) allows its constant to be chosen large enough to force a counterexample with \\(n\\geq j\\). Choose an increasing sequence of sizes. Its ordered unit-pair count is at least\n\\[\nn^{4/3-1/j}=t^{4-3/j},\n\\qquad t=n^{1/3}\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nThus it supplies configurations with \\(t^{4-o(1)}\\) ordered unit pairs.</p><p>Use two copies of each configuration as points and unit-circle centers. Proposition 1 specializes and restricts them to the incidence pieces used in the proof. Proposition 2 supplies prediction from short histories. Proposition 3 excludes a bounded subsequence of the arithmetic scale \\(S\\), hence gives \\(S\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>Proposition 4 constructs the rare/common decomposition, histories, templates, and score budgets. Proposition 5 gives \\(C_Q=o(S)\\), and Proposition 6 gives positive rare point-exception mass. Proposition 7 controls its large-height tail. Proposition 8 then supplies a graph on two copies of \\(P\\), with at least \\(t^{2-o(1)}\\) edges, scales \\(J\\to\\infty\\), assigned ratios satisfying\n\\[\n(z_p-z_r)(w_p-w_r)\n=2-\\lambda_{pr}-\\lambda_{pr}^{-1},\n\\qquad h(\\lambda_{pr})\\geq J/3,\n\\]\nuniformly negligible rectangle-defect heights, and the popular-unit-circle exclusion.</p><p>These are exactly the data excluded by Proposition 9. The contradiction rules out every sequence approaching exponent \\(4/3\\). Therefore some fixed exponent below \\(4/3\\) admits a uniform bound.</p><p>Increase that exponent to at least one if necessary, keeping it below \\(4/3\\). Enlarge the positive constant to cover remaining sizes \\(n\\geq2\\). Passing from ordered to unordered pairs changes only the constant. The cases \\(n=0,1\\) are immediate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 2.4. The additional conclusions below are the consequences established in original Lemmas 2.5–2.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assume a sequence of configurations of \\(n=t^3\\) distinct real planar points has at least \\(t^{4-o(1)}\\) ordered unit pairs.", "statement": "<p>Assume a sequence of configurations of \\(n=t^3\\) distinct real planar points has at least \\(t^{4-o(1)}\\) ordered unit pairs.</p><p>There are real algebraic point and center sets \\(P,Q\\), a nonempty unit-incidence graph \\(E\\subset P\\times Q\\), and a parameter \\(a=a(t)&gt;0\\), with\n\\[\na\\to0,\\qquad a\\log t\\to\\infty,\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n|P|=t^{1+2a+o(a)},\\qquad\n|Q|=t^{2+a+o(a)},\\qquad\n|E|=t^{2+2a+o(a)}.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nUniformly over vertices,\n\\[\nd(p)=t^{1+o(a)},\\qquad d(q)=t^{a+o(a)}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\mathbb P_E\\) be the uniform edge law, and let\n\\[\n\\mu(p)=\\frac{d(p)}{|E|},\\qquad\n\\kappa(q)=\\frac{d(q)}{|E|}.\n\\]\nWith\n\\[\n\\gamma=\\frac{27}{50},\\qquad \\eta=\\gamma-\\frac12=\\frac1{25},\n\\]\nthe marginals satisfy\n\\[\n\\mu(p)\\geq\\frac{\\gamma}{|P|},\\qquad\n\\kappa(q)\\geq\\frac{\\gamma}{|Q|}.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nThere is one sequence \\(\\xi_t\\to0\\), independent of all subsets, such that for \\(F\\subset P\\), \\(G\\subset Q\\), with\n\\[\nu=\\frac{|F|}{|P|},\\qquad v=\\frac{|G|}{|Q|},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_E(F,G)\\leq\\sqrt{uv}\\bigl((uv)^\\eta+\\xi_t\\bigr).\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nConsequently, writing \\(h=\\mu(F)\\), \\(k=\\kappa(G)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_E(F,G)\\leq C\\sqrt{hk}\\bigl((hk)^\\eta+\\xi_t\\bigr).\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]</p><p>The matching-label and uniformly rare-set conclusions of Lemmas 1.4 and 1.5 hold for these laws.</p><p>Define the wedge law by choosing \\(q\\sim\\kappa\\) and then choosing \\(p,r\\) independently and uniformly among its graph neighbors:\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(p,q,r)=\n\\begin{cases}\n(|E|d(q))^{-1},&amp;(p,q),(r,q)\\in E,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\nEach point marginal is \\(\\mu\\), and each point–center marginal is \\(\\mathbb P_E\\). If\n\\[\nd_{\\min,Q}=\\min_{q\\in Q}d(q),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(p,r)\\leq \\frac{2}{|E|d_{\\min,Q}}\n=t^{-2-3a+o(a)}\n\\quad(p\\ne r),\n\\tag{2.16}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(p=r)=\\frac{|Q|}{|E|}\n=t^{-a+o(a)}=o(1),\n\\tag{2.17}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nD:=D_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\bigl(\\mathcal L(p,r)\\,\\|\\,\\mu\\otimes\\mu\\bigr)\n=o(\\log t).\n\\tag{2.18}\n\\]\nHere, for finite laws \\(b,c\\),\n\\[\nD_{\\mathrm{KL}}(b\\|c)\n=\\sum_x b(x)\\log\\frac{b(x)}{c(x)},\n\\]\nwith the usual zero-summand and \\(+\\infty\\) conventions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 1.1 to each input configuration, retaining only the incidences under consideration. Choose \\(a\\to0\\) sufficiently slowly that the original count is at least \\(t^{4-o(a)}\\) and every fixed power of \\(\\log t\\) is \\(t^{o(a)}\\). In particular \\(a\\log t\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>Apply Lemma 1.2 with\n\\[\nR=t^{1-a},\\qquad s=O(t^{1-a}\\log t).\n\\]\nThere are \\(t^3\\) points and circles. Incidences on sampled circles number at most \\(st^3\\). Incidences at deleted points with other circles number at most \\(2st^3\\), since two circles meet at most twice. The deletion costs\n\\[\nO(t^{4-a}\\log t)=o(t^{4-o(a)}).\n\\]</p><p>Each remaining region meets at most \\(t^{2+a}\\) circles. Split its assigned point set into sets of size at most \\(2t^{1+2a}\\). The total number of pieces is at most\n\\[\nt^{2-2a+o(a)}.\n\\]\nOne piece has at least \\(t^{2+2a-o(a)}\\) incidences. Temporarily call its vertex sets \\(P_0,Q_0\\) and its edge count \\(e_0\\). Lemma 1.3 gives\n\\[\n|P_0|\\leq2t^{1+2a},\\quad\n|Q_0|\\leq t^{2+a},\\quad\nt^{2+2a-o(a)}\\leq e_0\\leq t^{2+2a+o(a)}.\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(b=o(a)\\) so slowly that \\(b\\log t\\to\\infty\\), and \\(b\\) dominates all preceding exponent errors and logarithmic factors. Delete point vertices of degree greater than \\(t^{1+b}\\) and center vertices of degree greater than \\(t^{a+b}\\), measuring degrees before deletion. Their counts are at most\n\\[\nt^{1+2a-b/2},\\qquad t^{2+a-b/2}.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 1.3 to each excessive set against the whole opposite side. The mixed term is at most\n\\[\nt^{2+2a-b/3+o(b)},\n\\]\nand the linear terms are bounded by\n\\[\nt^{1+2a+o(b)}+t^{2+a+o(b)}.\n\\]\nThus only \\(o(e_0)\\) edges are deleted. The retained graph has \\(t^{2+2a+o(a)}\\) edges and the required degree upper bounds. Dividing the edge count by these bounds shows that both vertex counts have their asserted scales. Relabel the trimmed graph \\((P_0,Q_0,E_0)\\).</p><p>For subsets of this graph, let \\(u,v\\) be counting fractions and \\(w\\) the captured edge fraction. Uniformly, with \\(T_t=t^{o(a)}\\),\n\\[\nw\\leq T_t\\bigl((uv)^{2/3}+t^{-a}v+t^{-1}u\\bigr),\n\\qquad\nw\\leq T_t\\min(u,v).\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nTaking minima separately in the linear terms and using \\(\\min(x,y)\\leq\\sqrt{xy}\\) gives\n\\[\nw\\leq T_t\\sqrt{uv}\\bigl((uv)^{1/6}+2t^{-a/2}\\bigr).\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\nFor example,\n\\[\n\\min(t^{-a}v,u)\\leq t^{-a/2}\\sqrt{uv},\n\\]\nand \\(t^{-1/2}\\leq t^{-a/2}\\) eventually.</p><p>Put \\(\\tau=t^{-a/8}\\). Among induced rectangles with \\(u,v\\geq\\tau\\), maximize\n\\[\n\\frac{w}{(uv)^\\gamma},\n\\qquad \\gamma=\\frac{27}{50}.\n\\]\nThe maximum is at least one. Let \\(U,V,W\\) be its counting and edge fractions. From (2.7),\n\\[\n1\\leq T_t\\left((UV)^{2/3-\\gamma}\n+2t^{-a/2}(UV)^{-\\eta}\\right).\n\\]\nFeasibility gives \\(UV\\geq t^{-a/4}\\), so the second term is at most\n\\[\n2t^{-49a/100+o(a)}=o(1).\n\\]\nIf \\(UV\\leq t^{-ca}\\) along a subsequence for any fixed \\(c&gt;0\\), the first term also tends to zero, since\n\\[\n2/3-\\gamma=19/150&gt;0.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nU,V,W\\geq t^{-o(a)}.\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]</p><p>Retain this maximizing rectangle as \\((P,Q,E)\\). Its scale estimates and degree upper bounds survive. For further subsets with fractions \\(u,v\\) within \\(P,Q\\), optimality gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_E(F,G)\\leq(uv)^\\gamma\n\\]\nwhenever \\(Uu,Vv\\geq\\tau\\). Otherwise\n\\[\nuv\\leq\\tau/\\min(U,V)=t^{-a/8+o(a)}.\n\\]\nApply (2.7) with fractions \\(Uu,Vv\\), divide by \\(W\\), and obtain\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_E(F,G)\n\\leq t^{o(a)}\\sqrt{uv}\\bigl((uv)^{1/6}+2t^{-a/2}\\bigr)\n\\leq\\xi_t\\sqrt{uv},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\xi_t=t^{-a/48+o(a)}\\to0\n\\]\nis uniform. This proves (2.5).</p><p>Removing any single vertex remains feasible eventually. Removing \\(p\\), optimality and concavity give\n\\[\n1-\\mu(p)\\leq(1-|P|^{-1})^\\gamma\n\\leq1-\\gamma|P|^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe center argument is identical. This proves (2.4), and multiplying by \\(|E|\\) gives the degree lower bounds.</p><p>Since \\(|F|/|P|\\leq\\mu(F)/\\gamma\\) and \\(|G|/|Q|\\leq\\kappa(G)/\\gamma\\), (2.9) follows. Lemmas 1.4–1.6 supply all remaining conclusions. In particular,\n\\[\n\\min_pd(p)\\to\\infty,\\qquad\n\\min_qd(q)\\to\\infty,\\qquad\n\\max_p\\mu(p)=t^{-1+o(1)}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\) be distinct points of \\(\\mathbb R^2\\), and let \\(E_0\\) be any collection of pairs \\((i,j)\\) for which \\( \\|x_i-x_j\\|_2=1. \\) There are distinct points \\(x'_1,\\ldots,x'_n\\), with real algebraic …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\) be distinct points of \\(\\mathbb R^2\\), and let \\(E_0\\) be any collection of pairs \\((i,j)\\) for which\n\\[\n\\|x_i-x_j\\|_2=1.\n\\]\nThere are distinct points \\(x'_1,\\ldots,x'_n\\), with real algebraic coordinates and arbitrarily close to the respective original points, such that\n\\[\n\\|x'_i-x'_j\\|_2=1\n\\qquad ((i,j)\\in E_0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a neighborhood of each original point and choose a rational open rectangle containing that point inside its neighborhood. For variables \\(X_i=(X_{i,1},X_{i,2})\\), impose membership in these rectangles, the equations\n\\[\n(X_{i,1}-X_{j,1})^2+(X_{i,2}-X_{j,2})^2=1\n\\qquad ((i,j)\\in E_0),\n\\]\nand the strict inequalities\n\\[\n(X_{i,1}-X_{j,1})^2+(X_{i,2}-X_{j,2})^2&gt;0\n\\qquad(i\\ne j).\n\\]\nThis finite system has rational coefficients and a real solution, namely the original configuration. The transfer principle for real closed fields [17, Theorems 3.1 and 4.1] gives a solution in the field of real algebraic numbers. The equations preserve the required unit pairs, the inequalities preserve distinctness, and the rectangles give the prescribed closeness. Extra unit pairs created by specialization are harmless.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(P_0\\) be a finite planar point set and let \\(\\mathcal Q\\) be a nonempty set of \\(l\\) distinct unit circles.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(P_0\\) be a finite planar point set and let \\(\\mathcal Q\\) be a nonempty set of \\(l\\) distinct unit circles. For \\(R\\geq1\\), sample\n\\[\ns=\\lceil CR\\log(2+l)\\rceil\n\\]\ncircles independently and uniformly with replacement, where \\(C\\) is a sufficiently large absolute constant.</p><p>With probability at least \\(9/10\\), after deleting the points on sampled circles, all remaining points can be assigned to at most \\(C'(1+s^2)\\) open regions, each intersecting at most \\(l/R\\) original circles. Each assigned point belongs to its assigned region.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an auxiliary horizontal direction before taking the sample. Require that an input point and an intersection point of two original circles have different horizontal coordinates unless they are the same point. Require also that an input point have horizontal coordinate different from that of a horizontal extremum of an original circle, unless it is that extremum. Only finitely many directions are excluded. For the latter assertion, if \\(c\\) is a circle center and \\(p\\) a point, equality with an extremal abscissa has the form\n\\[\ne\\cdot(p-c)=\\pm1\n\\]\nfor a unit horizontal direction \\(e\\), and has at most finitely many solutions in \\(e\\).</p><p>Ignore repetitions among the sampled circles. Express every sampled circle as its upper and lower semicircle graphs on their open horizontal domains. The event points are their horizontal extrema and all pairwise intersections, including tangencies. Between consecutive event abscissas the graphs have fixed vertical order; consecutive graphs, with infinite outer boundaries allowed, bound open gaps.</p><p>Sweep these gaps from left to right. Continue a gap across an event abscissa precisely when its two boundary graphs and their adjacency continue unchanged and its closed limiting vertical segment contains no event point. Otherwise start a new piece on the right. Each resulting piece is an open region between two fixed semicircle graphs, or infinite boundaries, over an open interval. It contains no part of a sampled circle.</p><p>An input point at an event abscissa must, by the direction choice, itself be an event point and therefore lie on a sampled circle. Thus every undeleted point lies in an open piece.</p><p>There are \\(O(1+s^2)\\) pieces, including with multiple intersections or several events at one abscissa. A new right-hand gap can start only if an event point lies in its closed limiting vertical segment. If \\(c_x\\) distinct sampled circles pass through an event point \\(x\\), only \\(O(1+c_x)\\) right-hand gaps can have this property. Extrema contribute \\(O(s)\\). At intersections, charge \\(c_x\\) to \\(2\\binom{c_x}{2}\\); every pair of circles has at most two intersections. The total cost is \\(O(s+s^2)\\), with only a constant number of initial unbounded gaps.</p><p>All possible pieces have descriptions from a bounded number of original circles: each horizontal endpoint is an extremal or intersection abscissa, or infinity, and each boundary graph is specified by a circle and a sign, or is infinite. At most\n\\[\nC_0(1+l)^6\n\\]\ncandidate regions can occur. For a fixed candidate intersecting more than \\(l/R\\) circles, the probability that no sampled circle intersects it is at most\n\\[\n(1-1/R)^s\\leq e^{-s/R},\n\\]\nwith the evident interpretation when \\(R=1\\). Every actual piece avoids the sample. A union bound, with \\(C\\) sufficiently large, gives failure probability at most \\(1/10\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Proposition 2.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(m\\) distinct planar points and \\(l\\) distinct unit circles, their number \\(I\\) of incidences satisfies \\( I\\leq C\\log^2(2+m+l)\\bigl(m^{2/3}l^{2/3}+m+l\\bigr). \\) In particular, when \\(m,l\\) are bounded by a fixed …", "statement": "<p>For \\(m\\) distinct planar points and \\(l\\) distinct unit circles, their number \\(I\\) of incidences satisfies\n\\[\nI\\leq C\\log^2(2+m+l)\\bigl(m^{2/3}l^{2/3}+m+l\\bigr).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nIn particular, when \\(m,l\\) are bounded by a fixed power of \\(t\\), the logarithmic factor is \\((\\log t)^{O(1)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Székely’s point–curve incidence theorem [25, Theorem 8] gives the stronger bound\n\\[\nI=O(m^{2/3}l^{2/3}+m+l)\n\\]\nwhen distinct curves meet in at most two points and distinct points belong to at most two curves. Distinct unit circles satisfy both hypotheses: possible unit-circle centers through two points are intersections of the two unit circles centered at those points.</p><p>If simple curves are taken to be arcs, cut each circle at two points outside the finite input point set. The resulting \\(2l\\) arcs satisfy the same pair bounds and preserve every incidence. The displayed weakened bound follows, including the empty-set cases.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume a sequence of finite edge laws has marginals \\(\\mu,\\kappa\\) and satisfies (2.5) and (2.9), with the fixed constants \\(\\gamma,\\eta>0\\) and \\(\\xi_t\\to0\\) from Proposition 1.", "statement": "<p>Assume a sequence of finite edge laws has marginals \\(\\mu,\\kappa\\) and satisfies (2.5) and (2.9), with the fixed constants \\(\\gamma,\\eta&gt;0\\) and \\(\\xi_t\\to0\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p>There are absolute constants \\(s_0&gt;0\\), \\(C&lt;\\infty\\), and an index after which the following holds uniformly for every pair of label maps\n\\[\n\\ell_P:P\\to A,\\qquad \\ell_Q:Q\\to A\n\\]\ninto a finite set. If\n\\[\ns=\\mathbb P_E(\\ell_P(p)\\ne\\ell_Q(q))\\leq s_0,\n\\]\nsome label \\(a_0\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mu\\{\\ell_P\\ne a_0\\}+\\kappa\\{\\ell_Q\\ne a_0\\}\\leq Cs.\n\\]\nMoreover, for independent \\(p,p'\\sim\\mu\\), at every value of \\(s\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(\\ell_P(p)\\ne\\ell_P(p'))\\leq Cs.\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\nThe analogous assertion holds for independent centers of law \\(\\kappa\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(u_i,v_i\\) be the counting fractions of the two classes labeled \\(i\\), including zero fractions. Sum (2.5) over matched rectangles. Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\sqrt{u_iv_i}\\leq1,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n1-s\\leq\\max_i(u_iv_i)^\\eta+\\xi_t.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small fixed \\(s_0\\) and sufficiently large \\(t\\), one label \\(a_0\\) has both counting fractions arbitrarily close to one. Apply (2.5) to either complementary class against the whole opposite side. The marginal exception masses\n\\[\nh=\\mu\\{\\ell_P\\ne a_0\\},\\qquad\nk=\\kappa\\{\\ell_Q\\ne a_0\\}\n\\]\nare as small as any prescribed fixed constant, uniformly over the label maps.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nJ=\\mathbb P_E(\\ell_P\\ne a_0,\\ell_Q\\ne a_0).\n\\]\nBy (2.9) and \\(\\sqrt{hk}\\leq(h+k)/2\\), the preceding constants can be chosen so that\n\\[\nJ\\leq(h+k)/4.\n\\]\nEdges with exactly one endpoint labeled \\(a_0\\) are mismatches, so\n\\[\ns\\geq h+k-2J\\geq\\frac12(h+k).\n\\]\nThis includes \\(s=0\\), with no additive error.</p><p>Two independent point labels differ with probability at most \\(2h\\). For \\(s&gt;s_0\\), enlarge \\(C\\) so that \\(Cs_0\\geq1\\). The center assertion is identical.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "Lemma 2.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assume (2.9), and let \\(\\delta_t\\to0\\) be any nonnegative sequence.", "statement": "<p>Assume (2.9), and let \\(\\delta_t\\to0\\) be any nonnegative sequence. The following estimates are uniform, with error factors depending only on \\(\\delta_t,\\xi_t\\), and the fixed constants in (2.9).</p><p>For \\(F\\subset P\\), \\(G\\subset Q\\), write \\(h=\\mu(F)\\), \\(k=\\kappa(G)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(h\\leq\\delta_t\\), with \\(k\\) arbitrary, then\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_E(F,G)\\leq\nC(\\delta_t^\\eta+\\xi_t)\\sqrt{hk}\n=o(1)\\sqrt{hk}.\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\nThe same holds with the sides interchanged.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If also \\(k\\leq\\delta_t\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_E(F,G)\\leq\n\\frac C2(\\delta_t^{2\\eta}+\\xi_t)(h+k)\n=o(1)(h+k).\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Under the wedge law (2.11), if \\(h\\leq\\delta_t\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(p\\in F,r\\in F)=o(1)h.\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]</p><p>The error factors may be taken outside sums or integrals over families satisfying the same marginal bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>The first two assertions follow directly from (2.9), using \\(hk\\leq\\delta_t\\) in the first case and \\(hk\\leq\\delta_t^2\\), \\(\\sqrt{hk}\\leq(h+k)/2\\) in the second.</p><p>For the third, put\n\\[\ng(q)=\\mathbb P(p\\in F\\mid q).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\mathbb E_\\kappa g=h\\), and the desired probability is \\(\\mathbb E_\\kappa g^2\\). If \\(h=0\\), there is nothing to prove. For \\(d\\in(0,1]\\), let\n\\[\nG_d=\\{q:g(q)&gt;d\\}.\n\\]\nMarkov gives \\(\\kappa(G_d)\\leq h/d\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_\\kappa g^2\n&amp;\\leq dh+\\mathbb E_\\kappa[g1_{G_d}]\\\\\n&amp;=dh+\\mathbb P_E(F,G_d)\\\\\n&amp;\\leq h\\left[d+Cd^{-1/2}\n\\left((\\delta_t^2/d)^\\eta+\\xi_t\\right)\\right].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nChoose \\(d=d_t\\to0\\) sufficiently slowly that\n\\[\n\\delta_t^{2\\eta}d_t^{-1/2-\\eta}\\to0,\n\\qquad\n\\xi_td_t^{-1/2}\\to0.\n\\]\nThe bracket tends to zero independently of \\(F\\), proving uniformity and the integrated form.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "Lemma 2.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a unit-incidence graph satisfying the size and degree estimates (2.2)–(2.3), use the wedge law (2.11), its point marginal \\(\\mu\\), and \\( d_{\\min,Q}=\\min_qd(q). \\) Then the atom, diagonal, and mutual-information …", "statement": "<p>For a unit-incidence graph satisfying the size and degree estimates (2.2)–(2.3), use the wedge law (2.11), its point marginal \\(\\mu\\), and\n\\[\nd_{\\min,Q}=\\min_qd(q).\n\\]\nThen the atom, diagonal, and mutual-information estimates (2.16)–(2.18) in Proposition 1 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(p,r\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(p,r)\n=\\frac1{|E|}\n\\sum_{q:\\,(p,q),(r,q)\\in E}\\frac1{d(q)}.\n\\]\nWhen \\(p\\ne r\\), there are at most two summands, giving (2.16). Summing on the diagonal gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(p=r)\n=\\frac1{|E|}\\sum_{q\\in Q}\\frac{d(q)}{d(q)}\n=\\frac{|Q|}{|E|}.\n\\]\nThe asymptotics follow from (2.2)–(2.3) and \\(a\\log t\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>For a finite random variable \\(X\\), write\n\\[\nH(X)=-\\sum_x\\mathbb P(X=x)\\log\\mathbb P(X=x).\n\\]\nEvery off-diagonal atom is at most\n\\[\nM_t=\\frac2{|E|d_{\\min,Q}}.\n\\]\nDropping nonnegative diagonal entropy contributions,\n\\[\nH(p,r)\\geq\n(1-\\mathbb P(p=r))\\log(1/M_t)\n\\geq(2-o(1))\\log t.\n\\]\nOn the other hand,\n\\[\nH(p)=H(r)\\leq\\log|P|=(1+o(1))\\log t.\n\\]\nExpanding relative entropy,\n\\[\n0\\leq D=H(p)+H(r)-H(p,r)=o(\\log t).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemma 3.1 and Corollaries 3.2–3.3, together with the sampling identities and parameter choice of Sections 3.2–3.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(P,Q\\) be finite nonempty sets and \\(\\omega\\) a probability law on \\(P\\times Q\\), with positive marginals \\(\\mu,\\kappa\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(P,Q\\) be finite nonempty sets and \\(\\omega\\) a probability law on \\(P\\times Q\\), with positive marginals \\(\\mu,\\kappa\\). Write \\(\\omega(p\\mid q)\\), \\(\\omega(q\\mid p)\\) for its conditional laws. Define\n\\[\n\\Omega(p,q,r)=\\kappa(q)\\omega(p\\mid q)\\omega(r\\mid q),\n\\qquad\n\\pi(p,r)=\\sum_q\\Omega(p,q,r),\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nD=D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(\\pi\\|\\mu\\otimes\\mu).\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nFor a history map \\(H:P\\to\\mathcal H\\), define, at positive-mass histories,\n\\[\n\\mu_h(x)=\n\\frac{\\mu(x)1_{\\{H(x)=h\\}}}{\\mu(H=h)}.\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nA fresh test is independent of the vertices and of the fixed history construction. Test maps and integrands are measurable.</p><p>The feature-prediction, finite-code acceptance, and strict-cross conclusions in Lemmas 2.1–2.3 hold. In particular:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For finite-code tests with alphabet size \\(M\\), history length parameter \\(K\\), and actual average rejection probability \\(s_0\\), a history with at most \\(M^{K-1}\\) values gives predicted rejection at most\n\\[\ns_0+4\\sqrt M(D/K)^{1/4}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For strict-cross tests with hashing parameter \\(L\\geq2\\), a history with at most \\(L^{2K}\\) values gives predicted failure at most\n\\[\ns_0+\\frac4L+18(D/K)^{1/4}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every fixed \\(H\\) and integer \\(r\\geq1\\), the coupling\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\{p,\\widehat p,q_1,\\ldots,q_r\\}\n=\\mu(p)\\mu_{H(p)}(\\widehat p)\\prod_{j=1}^r\\omega(q_j\\mid p)\n\\tag{3.13}\n\\]\nhas both point marginals \\(\\mu\\), each \\((p,q_j)\\)-marginal \\(\\omega\\), and each center marginal \\(\\kappa\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(p=\\widehat p)\n=\\sum_h\n\\frac{\\sum_{p:H(p)=h}\\mu(p)^2}{\\mu(H=h)}\n\\leq |H(P)|\\max_p\\mu(p).\n\\tag{3.14}\n\\]\nA union bound applies to the \\(r\\) acceptance comparisons using one common prediction and one common fresh test.</p><p>If \\(D=o(\\log t)\\), one can choose \\(K\\to\\infty\\), \\(K=o(\\log t)\\), \\(D/K\\to0\\). For a fixed alphabet this gives \\(M^K=t^{o(1)}\\). One can additionally choose \\(L\\to\\infty\\) so that\n\\[\nK\\log L=o(\\log t),\\qquad L^{2K}=t^{o(1)},\n\\]\nand impose finitely many further sufficiently slow growth requirements.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 2.1–2.3 prove the prediction conclusions.</p><p>For the coupling (3.13), sum over unused variables. The identity\n\\[\n\\sum_{p:H(p)=h}\\mu(p)\\mu_h(x)\n=\\mu(x)1_{\\{H(x)=h\\}}\n\\]\nshows that both point marginals are \\(\\mu\\); each edge and center marginal is as stated. Summing its diagonal gives (3.14). Conditional on \\(H(p)\\), the prediction is independent of all the neighbors. A union bound therefore bounds rejection in at least one of \\(r\\) comparisons by\n\\[\nr\\bigl(s_0+4\\sqrt M(D/K)^{1/4}\\bigr),\n\\]\nwithout independence of the rejection events.</p><p>Equivalently, draw \\(h\\) with probability \\(\\mu(H=h)\\), then draw two independent points from \\(\\mu_h\\). Once \\(H\\) is fixed, this pair law remains unchanged for every fresh test.</p><p>For the growth choices, put \\(T_0=\\log t\\) and take\n\\[\nK=\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{(D+1)T_0}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nThen \\(K\\to\\infty\\), \\(K=o(T_0)\\), and \\(D/K\\to0\\). For fixed \\(M\\), \\(M^K=t^{o(1)}\\). For hashing take\n\\[\nL=\\left\\lfloor\\exp\\sqrt{T_0/K}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nEventually \\(L\\geq2\\), \\(L\\to\\infty\\), and \\(K\\log L=o(T_0)\\).</p><p>If an additional sequence \\(\\eta_t\\to0\\) requires\n\\[\nkK\\log L=o(T_0),\\qquad k^{20}\\eta_t\\to0,\n\\]\nchoose\n\\[\nk=\\left\\lfloor\n\\min\\left\\{\n\\left(\\frac{T_0}{K\\log L}\\right)^{1/2},\n\\eta_t^{-1/40}\n\\right\\}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nFurther finitely many slow-growth requirements are imposed by decreasing this diverging sequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, “Prediction device.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the probability setting of Proposition 2, let \\(K,M\\geq1\\).", "statement": "<p>In the probability setting of Proposition 2, let \\(K,M\\geq1\\). Let \\(T_1,\\ldots,T_K\\) be independent tests with a common law. Each test specifies\n\\[\nc_T:P\\to[M],\\qquad\nf_T:[M]\\to\\mathbb R^d,\\qquad\ng_T:Q\\to\\mathbb R^d,\n\\]\nwhere \\(d\\geq1\\), and\n\\[\n\\|f_T(c)\\|\\leq B,\\qquad \\|g_T(q)\\|\\leq B'\n\\]\nfor all arguments.</p><p>Choose \\(J\\) uniformly from \\([K]\\), independently of the tests, and retain the strict prefix\n\\[\nH(p)=(c_{T_1}(p),\\ldots,c_{T_{J-1}}(p)).\n\\]\nAfter this prefix, draw a fresh test \\(T\\), and put\n\\[\nm_{T,h}=\\sum_x\\mu_h(x)f_T(c_T(x)).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{J,T_1,\\ldots,T_{J-1},T}\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E_{(p,q)\\sim\\omega}\n\\left\\langle\nf_T(c_T(p))-m_{T,H(p)},g_T(q)\n\\right\\rangle\n\\right|\n\\leq4BB'(D/K)^{1/4}.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nEvery realized history has at most \\(M^{K-1}\\) values. A strict-prefix history can be fixed before drawing the fresh test so that its average absolute discrepancy has this bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the chain rule for relative entropy [8, Theorem 2.5.3],\n\\[\nD_{\\mathrm{KL}}(a_{XY}\\|b_{XY})\n=\nD_{\\mathrm{KL}}(a_X\\|b_X)\n+\\sum_xa_X(x)D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(a_{Y\\mid x}\\|b_{Y\\mid x}),\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\ndata processing, and Pinsker’s inequality in the natural-logarithm convention,\n\\[\n2\\operatorname{TV}(a,b)^2\\leq D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(a\\|b).\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n|\\mathbb E_aF-\\mathbb E_bF|\n\\leq2\\|F\\|_\\infty\\operatorname{TV}(a,b).\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]</p><p>First freeze all \\(K\\) tests. Apply their complete transcript map to both coordinates of \\((p,r)\\). The actual law is the image of \\(\\pi\\), and the reference law is the image of \\(\\mu\\otimes\\mu\\). Their relative entropy is at most \\(D\\).</p><p>For step \\(j\\), let \\(H_j\\) be the strict prefix. Conditional on prefixes \\((h,h')\\), the reference next-code law is exactly\n\\[\nb_{j,h,h'}\n=(c_{T_j})_*\\mu_h\\otimes(c_{T_j})_*\\mu_{h'}.\n\\]\nLet \\(a_{j,h,h'}\\) be the actual conditional next-code law. Every actual positive-mass prefix pair has positive reference mass. Iterating the chain rule,\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^K\n\\mathbb E_{(p,r)\\sim\\pi}\nD_{\\mathrm{KL}}\n\\bigl(a_{j,H_j(p),H_j(r)}\n\\|b_{j,H_j(p),H_j(r)}\\bigr)\n\\leq D.\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nPinsker and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\\[\n\\frac1K\\sum_{j=1}^K\n\\mathbb E_\\pi\n\\operatorname{TV}\n\\bigl(a_{j,H_j(p),H_j(r)},\nb_{j,H_j(p),H_j(r)}\\bigr)\n\\leq\\sqrt{\\frac D{2K}}.\n\\tag{3.9}\n\\]</p><p>Subtract each endpoint’s own prediction:\n\\[\nU_j(p)=f_{T_j}(c_{T_j}(p))\n-\\sum_x\\mu_{H_j(p)}(x)f_{T_j}(c_{T_j}(x)).\n\\]\nIts norm is at most \\(2B\\). Under every reference conditional product, both residuals are centered and independent. Their inner product has absolute value at most \\(4B^2\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\frac1K\\sum_{j=1}^K\n\\mathbb E_\\pi\\langle U_j(p),U_j(r)\\rangle\n\\leq8B^2\\sqrt{\\frac D{2K}}.\n\\tag{3.10}\n\\]\nEvery summand is nonnegative, because conditional independence in the wedge gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\pi\\langle U_j(p),U_j(r)\\rangle\n=\n\\mathbb E_{q\\sim\\kappa}\n\\left\\|\\mathbb E_{p\\sim\\omega(\\cdot\\mid q)}U_j(p)\\right\\|^2.\n\\tag{3.11}\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz over \\(q\\), then over tests and steps, bounds the average absolute discrepancy by\n\\[\nB'\\left(8B^2\\sqrt{\\frac D{2K}}\\right)^{1/2}\n\\leq4BB'(D/K)^{1/4}.\n\\]</p><p>Average the initially frozen tests. The residual at step \\(j\\) uses only \\(T_1,\\ldots,T_j\\). Since the tests are independent and identically distributed, replacing \\(T_j\\) by a fresh test after the strict prefix does not change its joint law. Average over strict prefixes and fix one attaining at most the average. Its number of values is at most \\(M^{j-1}\\leq M^{K-1}\\).</p><p>The reference conditional law uses the first endpoint’s posterior at the first coordinate and the second endpoint’s posterior at the second. No independence of the actual endpoints conditional on their histories is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Corollary 3.2, “Finite-code acceptance.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 2’s probability setting, each fresh test specifies \\(c_T:P\\to[M]\\) and accepted-code sets \\(A_T(q)\\subset[M]\\).", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 2’s probability setting, each fresh test specifies \\(c_T:P\\to[M]\\) and accepted-code sets \\(A_T(q)\\subset[M]\\). Let\n\\[\ns_0=\\mathbb E_T\\mathbb P_{(p,q)\\sim\\omega}\n\\{c_T(p)\\notin A_T(q)\\}.\n\\]\nThere is a fixed strict-prefix history \\(H\\), with at most \\(M^{K-1}\\) values, such that\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_T\\sum_{p,q}\\omega(p,q)\n\\sum_x\\mu_{H(p)}(x)1_{\\{c_T(x)\\notin A_T(q)\\}}\n\\leq s_0+4\\sqrt M(D/K)^{1/4}.\n\\tag{3.12}\n\\]\nThe fresh test is independent of the fixed history construction.</p>", "proof": "<p>In Lemma 2.1 use\n\\[\nf_T(c)=e_c\\in\\mathbb R^M,\n\\]\nof norm one, and the acceptance-indicator vector as \\(g_T(q)\\), of norm at most \\(\\sqrt M\\). Actual and predicted failure probabilities differ by the negative of their acceptance probabilities. Average (3.7) and fix a strict prefix.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Corollary 3.3, “Prediction of strict crosses.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 2’s probability setting, let \\(K\\geq1\\), \\(L\\geq2\\).", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 2’s probability setting, let \\(K\\geq1\\), \\(L\\geq2\\). A fresh test specifies state maps\n\\[\ny_T(x)=(A_T(x),B_{1,T}(x))\n\\qquad(x\\in P\\cup Q)\n\\]\ninto a product of coordinate-label sets. Only finitely many attained labels are retained.</p><p>For \\(y=(a,b)\\), define its strict cross\n\\[\nR_y=\\{(a',b'):(a'=a,\\ b'\\ne b)\\ \\text{or}\\ (a'\\ne a,\\ b'=b)\\}.\n\\tag{3.15}\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\ns_0=\\mathbb E_T\\mathbb P_\\omega\n\\{y_T(p)\\notin R_{y_T(q)}\\}.\n\\]\nThere is a fixed history \\(H\\), with \\(|H(P)|\\leq L^{2K}\\), for which\n\\[\n\\nu_{h,T}=(y_T)_*\\mu_h\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_T\\mathbb E_\\omega\n[1-\\nu_{H(p),T}(R_{y_T(q)})]\n\\leq s_0+\\frac4L+18(D/K)^{1/4}.\n\\tag{3.16}\n\\]</p><p>If the test family has finite measure \\(W\\), and \\(m(\\tau)\\) bounds actual strict-cross failure at test \\(\\tau\\), a fixed history satisfies\n\\[\n\\int\n\\mathbb E_\\omega[1-\\nu_{H(p),\\tau}(R_{y_\\tau(q)})]\\,d\\tau\n\\leq\n\\int m(\\tau)\\,d\\tau\n+W\\bigl(4/L+18(D/K)^{1/4}\\bigr).\n\\tag{3.17}\n\\]\nFor \\(W=0\\), use the empty history.</p>", "proof": "<p>Augment every test by two fresh independent random maps from its attained coordinate labels into \\([L]\\), assigning independent uniform values to distinct labels. Numbering labels by first occurrence in a fixed vertex order makes this measurable. Use the same map on both sides for each coordinate and independent maps for different coordinates and tests.</p><p>The point code is its two hashed coordinates, so \\(M=L^2\\). For code \\((a,b)\\), use\n\\[\nf(a,b)=(e_a,e_b,e_{(a,b)})\n\\in\\mathbb R^L\\oplus\\mathbb R^L\\oplus\\mathbb R^{L^2},\n\\qquad \\|f(a,b)\\|=\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nFor hashed center state \\((a_q,b_q)\\), use\n\\[\ng(q)=(e_{a_q},e_{b_q},-2e_{(a_q,b_q)}),\n\\qquad \\|g(q)\\|=\\sqrt6.\n\\]\nTheir inner product is\n\\[\n1_{\\{a=a_q\\}}+1_{\\{b=b_q\\}}\n-2\\,1_{\\{(a,b)=(a_q,b_q)\\}},\n\\]\nthe hashed strict-cross indicator. Lemma 2.1 compares actual and predicted probabilities with error at most\n\\[\n4\\sqrt{18}(D/K)^{1/4}.\n\\]</p><p>Hashing preserves equalities and creates equality between two unequal labels with probability \\(1/L\\). For two fixed true states, their strict-cross indicator differs from its hashed indicator with probability at most \\(2/L\\). This applies to both actual and predicted pairs: conditional on the earlier prefix, current true test, and selected vertices, current hashes remain independent uniform maps. The predicted point uses no current hash.</p><p>Comparing actual true, actual hashed, predicted hashed, and predicted true failures gives extra error at most\n\\[\n4/L+4\\sqrt{18}(D/K)^{1/4}.\n\\]\nAverage the current test and hashes before fixing a prefix, and use \\(4\\sqrt{18}&lt;18\\). The true-state expression no longer involves current hashes. The history has at most\n\\[\n(L^2)^{K-1}\\leq L^{2K}\n\\]\nvalues.</p><p>For the integrated statement, sample the test from normalized measure \\(d\\tau/W\\) and multiply the result by \\(W\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 4.4 and Lemma 4.5, with the arithmetic framework of Lemmas 4.1–4.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use an incidence piece from Proposition 1.", "statement": "<p>Use an incidence piece from Proposition 1. For a point or center \\(x=(x_1,x_2)\\), put\n\\[\nz_x=x_1+ix_2,\\qquad w_x=x_1-ix_2.\n\\]\nFor an edge \\(e=(p,q)\\), define\n\\[\nd_e=z_p-z_q,\\qquad w_p-w_q=d_e^{-1}.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nAll edge jumps are nonzero, and distinct real points have distinct \\(z\\)- and \\(w\\)-coordinates.</p><p>Choose a finite normal number field \\(N\\) containing all coordinates and \\(i\\), and use the weighted absolute-value entries and absolute height \\(h\\) of Lemmas 3.1–3.2. A star denotes the corresponding weighted place sum.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nn_e(v)=-\\log|d_e|_v.\n\\]\nChoose finite intervals \\(I_v=[\\ell_v,u_v]\\), containing \\(0\\) and all \\(n_e(v)\\), and enlarged so that, for distinct \\(p,p'\\in P\\),\n\\[\n\\ell_v\\leq-\\log|z_p-z_{p'}|_v,\\qquad\nu_v\\geq\\log|w_p-w_{p'}|_v.\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nTake \\(I_v=[0,0]\\) at all but finitely many entries. Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\bigsqcup_v\\{v\\}\\times I_v,\n\\qquad\n\\int_{\\mathcal L}g\n=\\sum_v^*\\int_{\\ell_v}^{u_v}g(v,s)\\,ds.\n\\]\nAt a level \\((v,s)\\), set\n\\[\nY_e=1_{\\{n_e(v)&gt;s\\}},\\quad\n\\pi(v,s)=\\mathbb P_E(Y_e=1),\\quad\nf(v,s)=\\min(\\pi,1-\\pi),\\quad\nS=\\int_{\\mathcal L}f.\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n2\\mathbb E_{e,e'}h(d_e/d_{e'})\n=\n\\mathbb E_{e,e'}\\sum_v^*|n_e(v)-n_{e'}(v)|\n=\n2\\int\\pi(1-\\pi)\\leq2S,\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nwhere \\(e,e'\\) are independent uniform edges. Enlarging the level intervals does not change \\(S\\).</p><p>No subsequence has \\(S\\) bounded; hence \\(S\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>At each level assign a state\n\\[\ny_x=(A_x,B_{1,x}).\n\\]\nAt a nonarchimedean entry, these are the equivalence-class labels for\n\\[\n|z_x-z_{x'}|_v\\leq e^{-s},\n\\qquad\n|w_x-w_{x'}|_v\\leq e^s.\n\\]\nAt an archimedean entry belonging to \\(\\sigma:N\\to\\mathbb C\\), use square-grid cells for \\(\\sigma(z_x)\\), \\(\\sigma(w_x)\\), of side lengths \\(e^{-s}\\), \\(e^s\\), with fixed fractional shifts scaled across all levels of the entry.</p><p>An edge is consistent if exactly its \\(A\\)-labels agree when \\(Y_e=1\\), and exactly its \\(B_1\\)-labels agree when \\(Y_e=0\\). Define\n\\[\nm(v,s)=\\mathbb P_E(e\\text{ inconsistent}),\\qquad\nm_e=\\int1_{\\{e\\text{ inconsistent}\\}}.\n\\tag{4.17}\n\\]\nThe shifts can be fixed so that\n\\[\n\\int m=\\mathbb E_E m_e=O(1).\n\\tag{4.18}\n\\]\nAll level functions are measurable. Matching \\(A\\)-labels implies\n\\[\n|z_x-z_{x'}|_v\\leq c_ve^{-s},\n\\]\nand matching \\(B_1\\)-labels implies\n\\[\n|w_x-w_{x'}|_v\\leq c_ve^s,\n\\]\nwhere \\(c_v=1\\) at nonarchimedean entries and \\(c_v=\\sqrt2\\) at archimedean entries.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 supplies the normalized product formula, and Lemma 3.2 supplies the absolute height. For independent edges, threshold disagreement has integral\n\\[\n\\int|Y_e-Y_{e'}|\n=\\sum_v^*|n_e(v)-n_{e'}(v)|.\n\\]\nIts expectation is \\(2\\int\\pi(1-\\pi)\\), while the product formula identifies the place sum with \\(2h(d_e/d_{e'})\\). Since \\(\\pi(1-\\pi)\\leq f\\), this proves (4.9). Outside the range of the edge thresholds all bits agree, proving invariance under interval enlargement.</p><p>Lemma 3.4 excludes every bounded subsequence of \\(S\\), so \\(S\\to\\infty\\). Return to the original coordinates, and apply Lemma 3.5 to obtain the measurable state tests and integrated inconsistency bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, “Weighted absolute values.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a finite normal number field \\(N\\), let \\(d_N=[N:\\mathbb Q]\\).", "statement": "<p>For a finite normal number field \\(N\\), let \\(d_N=[N:\\mathbb Q]\\). There is a countable collection of absolute-value entries \\(v\\), each of weight \\(1/d_N\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The archimedean entries are \\(|u|_v=|\\sigma(u)|\\), one for each of the \\(d_N\\) embeddings \\(\\sigma:N\\to\\mathbb C\\); their total weight is one.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. All other entries are nonarchimedean.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every \\(u\\in N^\\times\\), only finitely many nonarchimedean entries satisfy \\(|u|_v\\ne1\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_v^*\\log|u|_v=0.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the classification of number-field places and product formula [19, Theorems 7.14–7.15]. At a finite place \\(\\mathfrak p\\) above a rational prime \\(\\ell\\), normalize the genuine absolute value by\n\\[\n|\\ell|_{\\mathfrak p}=\\ell^{-1},\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\nn_{\\mathfrak p}=[N_{\\mathfrak p}:\\mathbb Q_\\ell].\n\\]\nUse \\(n_{\\mathfrak p}\\) copies, each of weight \\(1/d_N\\). Milne’s local normalization formula [19, Lemma 8.6] converts his finite-place value to \\(|\\cdot|_{\\mathfrak p}^{n_{\\mathfrak p}}\\). His complex-place value is squared modulus; instead use ordinary modulus once for each embedding, counting conjugate embeddings separately. Take logarithms and divide by \\(d_N\\).</p><p>There are exactly \\(d_N\\) embeddings. The principal fractional ideal of a nonzero element has finitely many prime factors [19, Theorem 3.20], so all remaining finite-place values equal one.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, “Height inequalities.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the weighted entries of Lemma 3.1, define \\( h(0)=0,\\qquad h(u)=\\sum_v^*\\log^+|u|_v\\quad(u\\ne0), \\) where \\(\\log^+r=\\max(0,\\log r)\\) for \\(r>0\\), and \\(\\log^+0=0\\).", "statement": "<p>For the weighted entries of Lemma 3.1, define\n\\[\nh(0)=0,\\qquad\nh(u)=\\sum_v^*\\log^+|u|_v\\quad(u\\ne0),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\log^+r=\\max(0,\\log r)\\) for \\(r&gt;0\\), and \\(\\log^+0=0\\).</p><p>The height is independent of the chosen finite normal field and extensions of prime absolute values. For \\(u\\ne0\\),\n\\[\nh(u^{-1})=h(u),\\qquad\n\\sum_v^*|\\log|u|_v|=2h(u).\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nFor arbitrary \\(u,v\\),\n\\[\nh(uv)\\leq h(u)+h(v),\\qquad\nh(u+v)\\leq h(u)+h(v)+\\log2.\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]</p><p>If a random \\(X\\in N\\) is independent of a uniform embedding \\(\\sigma:N\\to\\mathbb C\\), then, for \\(B&gt;1\\), \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(|\\sigma(X)|&gt;B)\\leq\\frac{\\mathbb Eh(X)}{\\log B},\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(X\\ne0,\\ |\\sigma(X)|&lt;r)\n\\leq\\frac{\\mathbb Eh(X)}{\\log(1/r)}.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a finite extension \\(L/N\\), decomposition into completions [19, Proposition 8.2] gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathfrak q\\mid\\mathfrak p}[L_{\\mathfrak q}:N_{\\mathfrak p}]\n=[L:N].\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathfrak q\\mid\\mathfrak p}\n\\frac{[L_{\\mathfrak q}:\\mathbb Q_\\ell]}{[L:\\mathbb Q]}\n=\n\\frac{[N_{\\mathfrak p}:\\mathbb Q_\\ell]}{[N:\\mathbb Q]}.\n\\]\nThe normalized extended absolute values restrict to those on \\(N\\), so finite contributions are unchanged. Each embedding of \\(N\\) has \\([L:N]\\) extensions, proving the same at infinity. A common finite normal overfield compares any two choices. The place list includes all normalized extensions with local-degree multiplicities, so its enumeration adds no choice.</p><p>The product formula equates positive and negative logarithmic sums, giving (4.3). Multiplication gives the first inequality termwise. Addition costs at most \\(\\log2\\) at infinity and no constant at finite places; the total archimedean weight is one.</p><p>The average positive logarithm over embeddings is at most \\(h(X)\\). For \\(X\\ne0\\), the average negative logarithm is at most \\(h(X^{-1})=h(X)\\). Markov’s inequality gives both tail estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.3, “Transferring edge heights.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\omega\\) be a law on finite sets \\(P\\times Q\\), with point marginal \\(\\mu\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\omega\\) be a law on finite sets \\(P\\times Q\\), with point marginal \\(\\mu\\). Assume that, for all finite label assignments to the vertices, two independent \\(\\mu\\)-points disagree with probability at most \\(C_0\\) times the edge disagreement probability.</p><p>For every coordinate \\(U:P\\cup Q\\to N\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{p,p'\\sim\\mu}h(U_p-U_{p'})\n\\leq\nC_1\\bigl(\\mathbb E_{(p,q)\\sim\\omega}h(U_p-U_q)+1\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_1\\) depends only on \\(C_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(v\\) and \\(s\\geq0\\). At a nonarchimedean entry, give two vertices the same label if their \\(U\\)-coordinates differ by at most \\(e^s\\). The strong triangle inequality makes this an equivalence relation.</p><p>At infinity use a square grid in \\(\\mathbb C\\), of side \\(e^s\\), shifted by a uniform fractional vector. For two complex numbers at distance \\(a\\), mismatch probability is at most\n\\[\n\\min(1,\\sqrt2\\,ae^{-s}).\n\\]\nIndeed, the probability of mismatch in either real coordinate is at most its coordinate difference divided by the side, and the two differences sum to at most \\(\\sqrt2a\\). Integrating over \\(s\\geq0\\) gives at most \\(C(1+\\log^+a)\\).</p><p>At a finite place, the integrated mismatch is exactly\n\\[\n\\log^+|U_p-U_q|_v,\n\\]\nincluding zero differences. Thus the integrated edge mismatch, averaged over shifts, is at most\n\\[\nC\\bigl(\\mathbb E_\\omega h(U_p-U_q)+1\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>Apply the label hypothesis at each level and shift. At a finite place, point-pair mismatch is exactly \\(|U_p-U_{p'}|_v&gt;e^s\\). At infinity, distance greater than \\(\\sqrt2e^s\\) forces mismatch for every shift. Integrating bounds the point-pair height, with an additional \\(\\log\\sqrt2\\) from the total archimedean weight.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Proposition 4.4, “The bounded alternative is impossible.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the incidence pieces, level spaces, and quantities \\(S\\) in Proposition 3, no subsequence has \\(S\\) bounded.", "statement": "<p>For the incidence pieces, level spaces, and quantities \\(S\\) in Proposition 3, no subsequence has \\(S\\) bounded. Consequently \\(S\\to\\infty\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(S\\leq S_0\\) on a subsequence. Constants in this proof may depend on \\(S_0\\). From (4.9), choose an edge value \\(d_0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_Eh(d_e/d_0)\\leq S_0.\n\\]\nReplace coordinates temporarily by \\(z/d_0,d_0w\\). Their edge jumps are\n\\[\nD_e=d_e/d_0,\\qquad D_e^{-1},\n\\]\nwith bounded mean heights.</p><p>The matching-label conclusion of Proposition 1 supplies Lemma 3.3’s hypothesis. Apply it to both coordinates and average over the second point to choose \\(p_0\\in P\\) so that\n\\[\nZ_x=(z_x-z_{p_0})/d_0,\\qquad\nW_x=d_0(w_x-w_{p_0})\n\\]\nhave bounded point mean heights. On an edge,\n\\[\nZ_q=Z_p-D_{pq},\\qquad W_q=W_p-D_{pq}^{-1}.\n\\]\nLemma 3.2 bounds the center means. Thus, for a fixed \\(C_h\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\mathbb E_\\mu(h(Z_p)+h(W_p))\n+\\mathbb E_\\kappa(h(Z_q)+h(W_q))\\\\\n&amp;\\hspace{25mm}\n+\\mathbb E_E(h(D_e)+h(D_e^{-1}))\\leq C_h.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(4.10)\n\\]\nThe edge identity remains\n\\[\n(Z_p-Z_q)(W_p-W_q)=1.\n\\]</p><p>For any fixed finite history \\(H\\), use the coupling\n\\[\n\\mu(p)\\mu_{H(p)}(\\widehat p)\\prod_{j=1}^3\\mathbb P(q_j\\mid p)\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nand independently choose a uniform embedding \\(\\sigma\\). Both points have marginal \\(\\mu\\), each center has marginal \\(\\kappa\\), and every \\((p,q_j)\\) has the original edge law. Let \\(D_j=D_{pq_j}\\). Lemma 3.2 bounds\n\\[\n\\mathbb Eh(Z_{\\widehat p}-Z_p),\\qquad\n\\mathbb Eh(D_j-D_l)\n\\]\nusing (4.10), uniformly in \\(H\\).</p><p>Choose fixed \\(B\\geq2\\) so large that the chance any of\n\\[\nZ_p,W_p,Z_{\\widehat p},W_{\\widehat p},\n\\quad (Z_{q_j},W_{q_j},D_j,D_j^{-1})_{j=1}^3\n\\]\nhas embedded modulus above \\(B\\) is less than \\(1/100\\). Then choose fixed \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\) so small that the total probability of\n\\[\np\\ne\\widehat p,\\quad\n|\\sigma(Z_{\\widehat p}-Z_p)|&lt;r,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nD_j\\ne D_l,\\quad\n|\\sigma(D_j-D_l)|&lt;r\n\\qquad(j&lt;l)\n\\]\nis less than \\(1/100\\). The equalities are excluded from these small-difference events. These estimates hold for every fixed history.</p><p>Choose\n\\[\n0&lt;\\tau&lt;\\min\\left(1,\\frac{r^4}{128B^5}\\right).\n\\tag{4.12}\n\\]\nAt an embedding, quantize the real and imaginary parts of \\(Z_p,W_p\\) in \\([-B-1,B+1]\\), using half-open intervals of length at most \\(\\tau\\), with an overflow code outside the box. The alphabet size \\(M=M(B,\\tau)\\) is fixed. At \\(q\\), accept codes attained by actual graph neighbors. Every actual edge passes.</p><p>Proposition 1 gives \\(D=o(\\log t)\\). Choose \\(K\\to\\infty\\), \\(K=o(\\log t)\\), \\(D/K\\to0\\), and apply Proposition 2 to uniform embeddings. A fixed history with at most\n\\[\nM^{K-1}=t^{o(1)}\n\\]\nvalues gives predicted rejection \\(o(1)\\) at a fresh embedding. Use this history in (4.11). A union bound makes all three comparisons pass with probability \\(1-o(1)\\). Also\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(\\widehat p=p)\n\\leq |H(P)|\\max_p\\mu(p)=t^{-1+o(1)}.\n\\tag{4.13}\n\\]\nFor \\(j\\ne l\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(q_j=q_l)\\leq1/\\min_pd(p)=o(1).\n\\]\nDistinct centers have distinct \\(Z\\)-coordinates and hence distinct \\(D_j\\).</p><p>There is positive probability that all tests pass, all displayed quantities have modulus at most \\(B\\), and\n\\[\n|\\sigma(Z_{\\widehat p}-Z_p)|\\geq r,\\qquad\n|\\sigma(D_j-D_l)|\\geq r\\quad(j&lt;l).\n\\tag{4.14}\n\\]\nFix such an outcome and suppress \\(\\sigma\\).</p><p>For each \\(j\\), successful acceptance gives a genuine neighbor of \\(q_j\\) in the same nonoverflow code as \\(\\widehat p\\). Both complex-coordinate errors are at most \\(\\sqrt2\\tau\\). Comparing with its exact edge equation gives\n\\[\n|(Z_{\\widehat p}-Z_{q_j})(W_{\\widehat p}-W_{q_j})-1|\n\\leq8B\\tau.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\n\\Delta Z=Z_{\\widehat p}-Z_p,\\qquad\n\\Delta W=W_{\\widehat p}-W_p.\n\\]\nSubtract the equation at \\((p,q_j)\\):\n\\[\n\\Delta Z/D_j+\\Delta W D_j+\\Delta Z\\Delta W=E_j,\n\\qquad |E_j|\\leq8B\\tau.\n\\]\nSubtract the \\(j=1\\) equation from \\(j=2,3\\). The determinant is\n\\[\n\\det\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nD_2^{-1}-D_1^{-1}&amp;D_2-D_1\\\\\nD_3^{-1}-D_1^{-1}&amp;D_3-D_1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n=\n\\frac{(D_1-D_2)(D_1-D_3)(D_2-D_3)}{D_1D_2D_3}.\n\\tag{4.15}\n\\]\nIts modulus is at least \\(r^3/B^3\\). Matrix entries have modulus at most \\(2B\\), and right-hand sides at most \\(16B\\tau\\). Cramer’s rule gives\n\\[\n|\\Delta Z|\n\\leq\\frac{64B^5\\tau}{r^3}&lt;r/2,\n\\]\ncontradicting (4.14).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "Lemma 4.5, “State consistency.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the coordinate labels and level space in Proposition 3, the archimedean fractional shifts can be fixed so that \\( \\int m=\\mathbb E_E m_e=O(1). \\) At each nonarchimedean entry every edge is consistent outside …", "statement": "<p>For the coordinate labels and level space in Proposition 3, the archimedean fractional shifts can be fixed so that\n\\[\n\\int m=\\mathbb E_E m_e=O(1).\n\\]\nAt each nonarchimedean entry every edge is consistent outside finitely many threshold levels. The matching-label distance bounds and measurability assertions in Proposition 3 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a finite place, \\(n_e&gt;s\\) means\n\\[\n|d_e|_v&lt;e^{-s},\\qquad |d_e^{-1}|_v&gt;e^s,\n\\]\nso exactly the \\(A\\)-labels agree. For \\(n_e&lt;s\\), the conclusions reverse. Only \\(s=n_e\\), a null set, is excluded.</p><p>At infinity, put \\(r=|s-n_e|\\). The smaller coordinate jump divided by its grid scale is \\(e^{-r}\\), so mismatch probability under a uniform shift is at most \\(\\sqrt2e^{-r}\\). The larger jump divided by its scale is \\(e^r\\); it cannot be in one cell once \\(r\\geq\\log\\sqrt2\\). Thus expected inconsistency is at most\n\\[\n1_{\\{|s-n_e|\\leq\\log\\sqrt2\\}}+\\sqrt2e^{-|s-n_e|}.\n\\]\nIts integral over the real line is absolutely bounded. Sum over entries, whose total archimedean weight is one, and average over edges. Averaging fixes the finitely many archimedean shifts with the required bound.</p><p>The match implications follow from equivalence classes and the square diameter. Label finite-place classes by the first vertex in a fixed ordering, and use integer cell indices at infinity. All comparisons and indices are measurable functions of \\(s\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Propositions 5.5–5.6. This proposition also retains the parameter, exception, history, and template constructions of original Lemmas 5.1–5.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the incidence graph of Proposition 1 and the original coordinate levels and states of Proposition 3, with \\(S\\to\\infty\\).", "statement": "<p>Use the incidence graph of Proposition 1 and the original coordinate levels and states of Proposition 3, with \\(S\\to\\infty\\). Let\n\\[\nd_{P,\\min}=\\min_pd(p),\\qquad d_{Q,\\min}=\\min_qd(q).\n\\]\nChoose integers \\(K,L,k\\to\\infty\\) so that\n\\[\nK\\log L=o(\\log t),\\qquad D/K=o(1),\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\\[\nkK\\log L=o(\\log t),\\qquad\nk^{20}\\bigl((D/K)^{1/4}+L^{-1}+S^{-1}+d_{Q,\\min}^{-1}\\bigr)=o(1).\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\n\\epsilon=k^{-1/16},\\qquad e_t=(D/K)^{1/4}+L^{-1}.\n\\]\nDefine common, damaged, and good rare levels by\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\{f\\geq\\epsilon\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal B=\\{f&lt;\\epsilon,\\ m&gt;1/k\\},\\quad\n\\mathcal G=\\{f&lt;\\epsilon,\\ m\\leq1/k\\}.\n\\]\nTheir widths satisfy\n\\[\nW_c=\\int1_{\\mathcal C}\\leq S/\\epsilon,\\qquad\nW_b=\\int1_{\\mathcal B}=O(k)=o(S).\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]</p><p>At a good rare level, let \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) be the minority bit. The majority-matching coordinate is \\(B_1\\) when \\(r=1\\), and \\(A\\) when \\(r=0\\). Choose a dominant label \\(\\ell\\) for this coordinate as in Lemma 4.2. Let \\(I_x\\) indicate that vertex \\(x\\) does not have this label, and define\n\\[\nh_P=\\mathbb E_\\mu I_p,\\qquad\nh_Q=\\mathbb E_\\kappa I_q,\\qquad\nH_x^*=\\int_{\\mathcal G}I_x.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nh_P+h_Q\\leq C(f+m)=o(1),\\qquad\nf\\leq h_P+h_Q+m,\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*+\\mathbb E_\\kappa H_q^*=O(S),\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nand the joint-exception estimates in Lemma 4.2 hold.</p><p>There is a fixed history \\(H\\), with \\(|H(P)|\\leq L^{2K}\\), such that, writing \\(\\nu_h\\) for the true-state law under \\(\\mu_h\\) at a fresh level,\n\\[\nk^3\\int_{\\mathcal C}\n\\mathbb E_E[1-\\nu_{H(p)}(R_{y_q})]=o(S).\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nFor a center \\(q\\), a template \\(\\theta=(h_1,\\ldots,h_k)\\) is obtained by sampling \\(k\\) independent neighbors of \\(q\\) and recording their histories. Write\n\\[\na(q,\\theta)=\\mathbb P(\\theta\\mid q),\\qquad\nM\\leq L^{2Kk}=t^{o(1)}\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nfor the template law and number of possible templates. Use\n\\[\n\\omega(p,q,\\theta)=\\frac{1_{\\{(p,q)\\in E\\}}}{|E|}a(q,\\theta).\n\\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nThe point and template are independent conditional on \\(q\\).</p><p>Consider three fixed pair experiments:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Q\\): choose \\(q\\sim\\kappa\\), then independent \\(p,r\\mid q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\): choose \\(p\\sim\\mu\\), then independent \\(q,q'\\mid p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\theta\\): choose \\((p,\\theta)\\) from its \\(\\omega\\)-marginal, then independent \\(q,q'\\mid(p,\\theta)\\).</p><p>Write \\(x\\) for the star vertex, \\(y_1,y_2\\) for opposite endpoints, \\(Y_1,Y_2\\) for edge bits, and\n\\[\n\\rho=\\mathbb P(Y_1=1\\mid\\text{base}),\n\\]\nwhere the base includes \\(\\theta\\) in the third experiment. Put\n\\[\nC_i=\\int_{\\mathcal C}\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm{base}}[2\\rho(1-\\rho)],\n\\qquad i\\in\\{Q,P,P\\theta\\}.\n\\tag{5.10}\n\\]</p><p>When \\(Y_1=Y_2=1\\), let \\(N\\) mean that the \\(B_1\\)-labels of \\(y_1,y_2\\) match; when \\(Y_1=Y_2=0\\), let \\(N\\) mean that their \\(A\\)-labels match; otherwise \\(N\\) is false. Set\n\\[\n\\delta=\\frac1{100},\\qquad \\lambda=\\delta^2/2,\n\\]\n\\[\nc(\\rho)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1+\\lambda,&amp;|\\rho-\\tfrac12|\\leq\\delta,\\\\\n1-\\lambda,&amp;|\\rho-\\tfrac12|&gt;\\delta.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nDefine the level score as\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2I_x1_{\\{Y_1=Y_2=r,\\ N\\}},&amp;\\mathcal G,\\\\\nc(\\rho)1_{\\{Y_1=Y_2,\\ N\\}},&amp;\\mathcal C,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\mathcal B,\n\\end{cases}\n\\quad-\\ 1_{\\{Y_1\\ne Y_2\\}}.\n\\]\nLet \\(D_i\\) be its expectation integrated over all levels.</p><p>For each experiment,\n\\[\nD_i\\leq o(S)+10\\delta^2\\lambda C_i.\n\\tag{5.13}\n\\]\nIts score integrated outside \\(\\mathcal C\\) is at least\n\\[\n2\\mathbb EH_x^*-2\\mathbb EH_y^*-o(S),\n\\tag{5.16}\n\\]\nusing the original edge marginals. The main term is\n\\[\n2\\mathbb E_\\kappa H_q^*-2\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*\n\\]\nfor \\(Q\\), and its negative for \\(P\\) and \\(P\\theta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 chooses the parameters. The width bounds follow from\n\\[\nW_c\\leq S/\\epsilon,\\qquad\nW_b\\leq k\\int m=O(k)=o(S).\n\\]\nAt each place \\(\\mathcal C\\) is an interval because \\(s\\mapsto\\mathbb P_E(n_e(v)&gt;s)\\) is nonincreasing.</p><p>Lemma 4.2 supplies the dominant-label exception bounds. Apply Proposition 2’s integrated strict-cross prediction to common levels, normalized by \\(W_c\\) when \\(W_c&gt;0\\). Inconsistency bounds actual strict-cross failure. The resulting history satisfies\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal C}\\mathbb E_E[1-\\nu_{H(p)}(R_{y_q})]\n\\leq\\int_{\\mathcal C}m+O(W_ce_t).\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(k^3\\), with Lemma 4.1, gives (5.7). If \\(W_c=0\\), take the empty history.</p><p>The fixed history gives at most\n\\[\n|H(P)|^k\\leq L^{2Kk}=t^{o(1)}\n\\]\ntemplates. Their construction uses no fresh level and makes the point independent of the template conditional on the center.</p><p>Lemma 4.3 establishes the sampling and collision laws. Lemmas 4.5 and 4.6 give the upper and lower budgets.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, “Parameter choice.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the incidence pieces with \\(D=o(\\log t)\\), \\(d_{Q,\\min}\\to\\infty\\), and \\(S\\to\\infty\\), integers \\(K,L,k\\to\\infty\\) satisfying (5.1)–(5.2) exist.", "statement": "<p>For the incidence pieces with \\(D=o(\\log t)\\), \\(d_{Q,\\min}\\to\\infty\\), and \\(S\\to\\infty\\), integers \\(K,L,k\\to\\infty\\) satisfying (5.1)–(5.2) exist. They also satisfy\n\\[\nk^3/S=o(1),\\qquad\nk^{3+1/16}e_t=o(1),\\qquad\nk=t^{o(1)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First take\n\\[\nK=\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{(D+1)\\log t}\\right\\rceil,\n\\]\nand then choose \\(L\\to\\infty\\) with\n\\[\n\\log L\\sim\\sqrt{\\log t/K}.\n\\]\nThese give (5.1). Both \\(K\\log L/\\log t\\) and the parenthesized expression in (5.2) tend to zero. Choose \\(k\\to\\infty\\) sufficiently slowly. The stated consequences follow directly; in particular \\(k=o(\\log t)\\), since \\(K\\log L\\to\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “Exception mass and joint exceptions.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the good rare levels \\(\\mathcal G\\) and dominant-coordinate exception indicators in Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>Use the good rare levels \\(\\mathcal G\\) and dominant-coordinate exception indicators in Proposition 4.</p><p>The dominant label can be chosen measurably so that (5.4)–(5.5) hold. Under the original edge and ordinary wedge laws,\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal G}\\mathbb P_E(I_p=I_q=1)=o(S),\n\\qquad\n\\int_{\\mathcal G}\\mathbb P(I_p=I_r=1)=o(S).\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At a good rare level, the majority-matching coordinate has edge mismatch probability at most \\(f+m\\). The matching-label conclusion of Proposition 1 supplies a label \\(\\ell\\) with total marginal exception probability \\(O(f+m)\\). Fixed vertex orderings settle choices measurably. Uniformly,\n\\[\nh_P+h_Q\\leq C(\\epsilon+1/k)=o(1).\n\\]</p><p>If both endpoints have the dominant label, a consistent edge has majority bit. Thus every minority edge has an exceptional endpoint or is inconsistent:\n\\[\nf\\leq h_P+h_Q+m.\n\\]\nIntegrating the first bound and using \\(\\int m=O(1)\\), \\(S\\to\\infty\\), gives (5.5).</p><p>Apply Proposition 1’s rare-set estimates with uniform marginal bound\n\\[\na_t=C(\\epsilon+k^{-1})\\to0.\n\\]\nThe edge joint probability is \\(o(1)(h_P+h_Q)\\), and the wedge joint probability is \\(o(1)h_P\\). Both multipliers are uniform over \\(\\mathcal G\\), so integration and (5.5) give (5.6).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, “Marginals and diagonal pairs.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the three pair experiments in Proposition 4, every edge slot, after averaging its base, has the original uniform law on \\(E\\).", "statement": "<p>For the three pair experiments in Proposition 4, every edge slot, after averaging its base, has the original uniform law on \\(E\\).</p><p>For each \\(p\\), the collision probability \\(q=q'\\) in the \\(P\\theta\\) experiment, averaged over \\(\\theta\\mid p\\), is at most \\(M/d(p)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The first assertion is immediate for ordinary stars. For the third experiment, summing (5.9) over templates gives the original edge law; conditional sampling preserves it.</p><p>A positive-mass template slice of weight\n\\[\nw_\\theta=\\sum_{p,q}\\omega(p,q,\\theta)\n\\]\nhas one-edge law \\(\\omega(p,q,\\theta)/w_\\theta\\), averaged with weight \\(w_\\theta\\). A template may also be attached independently at a \\(Q\\)-base: conditional on \\((q,\\theta)\\), the two points still have their original neighbor law. Its single-slot slice law agrees with that for \\(P\\theta\\).</p><p>Fix \\(p\\), and put \\(a_p(\\theta)=\\mathbb P(\\theta\\mid p)\\). For \\(a_p(\\theta)&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\omega(q\\mid p,\\theta)\n=\n\\frac{1_{\\{(p,q)\\in E\\}}a(q,\\theta)}\n{d(p)a_p(\\theta)}\n\\leq\\frac1{d(p)a_p(\\theta)}.\n\\]\nCollision probability is at most the largest atom. Multiply by \\(a_p(\\theta)\\) and sum over at most \\(M\\) positive-mass templates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.4, “Two intervals and a central window.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(b\\leq b'\\), \\(T\\geq0\\), and let \\(J\\) be an interval of finite length, with arbitrary endpoint conventions.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(b\\leq b'\\), \\(T\\geq0\\), and let \\(J\\) be an interval of finite length, with arbitrary endpoint conventions. Put\n\\[\nI_-=[b-T,b],\\qquad I_+=[b',b'+T],\n\\qquad a=|J\\cap I_-|+|J\\cap I_+|.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\bigl[(1-\\lambda)2T+2\\lambda a-2T\\bigr]_+\n\\leq\\lambda|J|1_{\\{b,b'\\in\\operatorname{int}J\\}}.\n\\tag{5.11}\n\\]\nIf a real random variable \\(X\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|\\mathbb P(X&gt;s)-1/2|\\leq\\delta\n\\qquad(s\\in J),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(X\\in\\operatorname{int}J)\\leq2\\delta.\n\\tag{5.12}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The left side of (5.11) is \\([2\\lambda(a-T)]_+\\). Positivity requires both intersections to have positive length, since either has length at most \\(T\\). Since \\(J\\) is an interval, this puts both \\(b,b'\\) in its interior, also when \\(b=b'\\).</p><p>Moreover \\(a\\leq2T\\) and \\(a\\leq|J|\\), since \\(I_-,I_+\\) have disjoint interiors. Hence\n\\[\n2(a-T)\\leq a\\leq|J|.\n\\]</p><p>For (5.12), if \\(u&lt;v\\) are interior points,\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(u&lt;X\\leq v)\n=\\mathbb P(X&gt;u)-\\mathbb P(X&gt;v)\\leq2\\delta.\n\\]\nExhaust the open interior by these intervals. The empty-interior case is immediate, and no condition on endpoint atoms is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "Proposition 5.5, “Upper score budget.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the score setting of Proposition 4, each \\(i\\in\\{Q,P,P\\theta\\}\\) satisfies \\( D_i\\leq o(S)+10\\delta^2\\lambda C_i. \\)", "statement": "<p>In the score setting of Proposition 4, each \\(i\\in\\{Q,P,P\\theta\\}\\) satisfies\n\\[\nD_i\\leq o(S)+10\\delta^2\\lambda C_i.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a base and two distinct opposite endpoints, with incident jumps \\(d,d'\\). They are nonzero and distinct. At place \\(v\\), put\n\\[\nb=\\min(n_e(v),n_{e'}(v)),\\qquad\nb'=\\max(n_e(v),n_{e'}(v)),\n\\]\n\\[\nu=-\\log|d-d'|_v,\\qquad \\alpha=u-b.\n\\]\nThe opposite-endpoint coordinate differences are \\(d-d'\\), \\(1/d-1/d'\\), up to signs, so\n\\[\n-\\log|(d-d')(1/d-1/d')|_v\n=2\\alpha-(b'-b).\n\\tag{5.14}\n\\]\nThe bit-difference charge integrates to \\(b'-b\\).</p><p>Let \\(c_v=\\log2\\) at archimedean entries and zero otherwise. The triangle inequality gives \\(\\alpha\\geq-c_v\\); put \\(T_v=\\alpha+c_v\\geq0\\).</p><p>A positive charge with both bits one requires \\(s&lt;b\\) and a \\(B_1\\)-match, giving\n\\[\ns\\geq b'-\\alpha-c_v\\geq b-T_v.\n\\]\nWith both bits zero, an \\(A\\)-match gives\n\\[\ns\\geq b',\\qquad s\\leq b+\\alpha+c_v\\leq b'+T_v.\n\\]\nThus positive charges lie in\n\\[\nI_-=[b-T_v,b],\\qquad I_+=[b',b'+T_v].\n\\tag{5.15}\n\\]</p><p>Suppose a rare positive with minority bit one is counted at \\(s_0\\). Then \\(s_0&lt;b\\) and \\(\\mathbb P_E(n_e(v)&gt;s_0)&lt;\\epsilon\\). By monotonicity, all common levels and all minority-zero levels lie below \\(s_0\\); none can produce a positive in \\(I_+\\). Every minority-one positive lies in \\(I_-\\). The minority-zero case is symmetric. Thus, if any rare positive is counted, all positive charges are on one side, have coefficients at most two, and total at most \\(2T_v\\).</p><p>Otherwise only common positives remain. For this base, let\n\\[\nJ_v=\\{s:(v,s)\\in\\mathcal C,\\ |\\rho(v,s)-1/2|\\leq\\delta\\}.\n\\]\nThis is an interval because the conditional edge law is fixed across levels. Extend coefficient \\(1-\\lambda\\) to both full intervals in (5.15), increasing it by \\(2\\lambda\\) on \\(J_v\\). Lemma 4.4 bounds excess above \\(2T_v\\) by\n\\[\n\\lambda|J_v|\n1_{\\{n_e(v),n_{e'}(v)\\in\\operatorname{int}J_v\\}}.\n\\]\nThis is also a valid excess bound in the rare-positive case.</p><p>Combine with the negative charge and (5.14), and sum over places. The product formula cancels the logarithm of\n\\[\n(d-d')(1/d-1/d'),\n\\]\nleaving allowance \\(2\\sum_v^*c_v=2\\log2\\). Zero-width entries can be included: their charges vanish and the same upper bound holds.</p><p>Average over the fixed-base independent pair law, restricting to distinct endpoints. Drop that restriction only in the nonnegative excess term. Lemma 4.4 bounds the expected indicator by \\(4\\delta^2\\). On \\(J_v\\),\n\\[\n2\\rho(1-\\rho)\\geq2(1/4-\\delta^2).\n\\]\nThe distinct-pair contribution is at most\n\\[\nO(1)+\n\\frac{4\\delta^2\\lambda}{2(1/4-\\delta^2)}C_i\n\\leq O(1)+10\\delta^2\\lambda C_i.\n\\]</p><p>For equal endpoints, positive score is at most \\(2(W_c+H_x^*)\\), and negative score is zero. Ordinary collision probabilities are \\(1/d(q)\\), \\(1/d(p)\\); Lemma 4.3 gives \\(M/d(p)\\) pointwise in \\(p\\) for templated stars. Their integrated contributions are at most\n\\[\n\\frac2{d_{Q,\\min}}(W_c+\\mathbb E_\\kappa H_q^*),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac2{d_{P,\\min}}(W_c+\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*),\n\\qquad\n\\frac{2M}{d_{P,\\min}}(W_c+\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*).\n\\]\nAll are \\(o(S)\\), using (5.1)–(5.5), \\(M=t^{o(1)}\\), and \\(d_{P,\\min}=t^{1+o(a)}\\). The pointwise collision bound permits multiplication by the unbounded \\(H_p^*\\) using only its first moment. Finally \\(O(1)=o(S)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.6", "orig": "Proposition 5.6, “Rare-level lower budget.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For any of the three pair experiments in Proposition 4, with star vertex \\(x\\) and opposite endpoint \\(y\\), the score integrated outside \\(\\mathcal C\\) is at least \\( 2\\mathbb EH_x^*-2\\mathbb EH_y^*-o(S), \\) with …", "statement": "<p>For any of the three pair experiments in Proposition 4, with star vertex \\(x\\) and opposite endpoint \\(y\\), the score integrated outside \\(\\mathcal C\\) is at least\n\\[\n2\\mathbb EH_x^*-2\\mathbb EH_y^*-o(S),\n\\]\nwith expectations under the original edge marginals.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a good rare level, let \\(M_j\\) be the inconsistency indicator in slot \\(j\\). If \\(I_x=1\\), both opposite endpoints are normal, and both edges consistent, their dominant-coordinate labels match each other but differ from the base’s. Both bits are therefore minority bits, and the required nonshared coordinate matches. The positive score is at least\n\\[\n2I_x-2I_xI_{y_1}-2I_xI_{y_2}-2M_1-2M_2.\n\\]\nIf both opposite endpoints are normal and both edges consistent, their bits agree regardless of whether the base is exceptional. The negative charge is at most\n\\[\nI_{y_1}+I_{y_2}+M_1+M_2.\n\\]</p><p>Average. Each slot has the original edge marginal by Lemma 4.3, including with templates. Joint terms \\(I_xI_{y_j}\\) integrate to \\(o(S)\\) by Lemma 4.2, and inconsistency integrates to \\(O(1)\\). This proves the bound on \\(\\mathcal G\\). On \\(\\mathcal B\\), the score is at least \\(-1\\), costing \\(W_b=o(S)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 6.3, “Common variation.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the incidence graph and coordinate levels of Propositions 1 and 3, and the parameters, fixed history, templates, and scores of Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>Use the incidence graph and coordinate levels of Propositions 1 and 3, and the parameters, fixed history, templates, and scores of Proposition 4. In particular, \\(S\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\n\\epsilon=k^{-1/16},\\qquad W_c\\leq k^{1/16}S,\n\\]\nand the fixed history satisfies (5.7).</p><p>For the center-star and point–template-star experiments,\n\\[\nC_Q+C_{P\\theta}=o(S).\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nTheir rare exception means satisfy\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\kappa H_q^*\n\\leq\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*+o(S).\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]</p><p>For use in the proof, at each common level define\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\nu_q=\\mathbb E_{p\\mid q}\\nu_{H(p)}\n\\]\nand, for template \\(\\theta=(h_1,\\ldots,h_k)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal Y_\\theta\n=\n\\{y\\text{ an attained center state}:\n\\nu_{h_j}(R_y)\\geq1-k^{-2}\\text{ for all }j\\}.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nA row has fixed first coordinate, and a column fixed second coordinate.</p>", "proof": "<p>The actual center state is usually a candidate. A union bound and Markov give\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal C}\\mathbb P(y_q\\notin\\mathcal Y_\\theta)\n\\leq\nk^3\\int_{\\mathcal C}\n\\mathbb E_E[1-\\nu_{H(p)}(R_{y_q})]\n=o(S).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nLemma 5.1’s failure probability also integrates to \\(o(S)\\), since \\(W_c\\leq k^{1/16}S\\).</p><p>Outside that failure, the intersection of any at most three selected candidate crosses has \\(\\widetilde\\nu_q\\)-mass at least \\(1-3k^{-1/4}\\). If the intersection is contained in a row or column, take that row or column as support; if it has at most two states, take the intersection. Apply Lemma 5.2 with \\(\\eta_0=k^{-1/8}\\). Its integrated cost is at most\n\\[\n8k^{-1/8}W_c\\leq8k^{-1/16}S=o(S).\n\\]\nAn empty intersection cannot have the asserted mass eventually.</p><p>The strict-cross geometry is as follows. For two distinct states in one row,\n\\[\nR_{(a,b)}\\cap R_{(a,b')}\n=\\{(a,v):v\\notin\\{b,b'\\}\\}.\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nA third state outside that row cuts this to at most one point. The column assertion is identical. For states differing in both coordinates,\n\\[\nR_{(a,b)}\\cap R_{(a',b')}\n=\\{(a,b'),(a',b)\\}.\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nThree states with pairwise distinct first coordinates and pairwise distinct second coordinates have empty common cross.</p><p>Classify \\(\\mathcal Y_\\theta\\) as empty, singleton, containing a shared-coordinate pair, or having pairwise distinct coordinates. In the shared-coordinate case, either all candidates lie in that row or column or a third lies outside. In the pairwise-distinct case, there are two candidates or at least three. Select the corresponding two or three witnesses by fixed ordering.</p><p>Declare an edge with template bad if it is inconsistent, its center state is not a candidate, Lemma 5.1’s failure event occurs, or the restricted-support transfer fails for these witnesses. Empty candidate sets and empty witness intersections have no good edges. The preceding bounds give\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal C}\\mathbb P(p,q,\\theta\\text{ bad})=o(S).\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]</p><p>The full conditional bit probability \\(\\rho\\), not a law renormalized after deleting bad edges, is used throughout. For all \\(\\rho\\in[0,1]\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;c(\\rho)(\\rho^2+(1-\\rho)^2)-2\\rho(1-\\rho)\\\\\n&amp;\\quad=(2\\rho-1)^2\n+(c(\\rho)-1)(\\rho^2+(1-\\rho)^2)\n\\geq\\lambda/2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(6.7)\n\\]\nInside the central window, use \\(\\rho^2+(1-\\rho)^2\\geq1/2\\); outside, use \\(4\\delta^2-\\lambda&gt;\\lambda/2\\).</p><p>Fix a common level. For a positive-mass template let\n\\[\n\\pi_\\theta=\\mathbb P(\\theta),\\qquad\nb_\\theta=\\mathbb P(\\text{bad edge}\\mid\\theta).\n\\]\nIn the \\(Q\\)-experiment conditioned on \\(\\theta\\), sample the center by its actual conditional law, then independent ordinary neighbors. In the \\(P\\theta\\)-experiment, sample the point conditional on \\(\\theta\\), then independent centers conditional on \\((p,\\theta)\\). Each slot has the same law \\(\\mathbb P(p,q\\mid\\theta)\\). A pair with a bad slot has probability at most \\(2b_\\theta\\).</p><p>Let \\(V_Q(\\theta),V_{P\\theta}(\\theta)\\) be the slice averages of \\(2\\rho(1-\\rho)\\), and \\(d_Q(\\theta),d_{P\\theta}(\\theta)\\) the common scores. Each variation is at most \\(1/2\\), and each score is at least minus its variation. We establish\n\\[\nd_Q(\\theta)+d_{P\\theta}(\\theta)\n\\geq\\frac{\\lambda}{4}\n\\bigl(V_Q(\\theta)+V_{P\\theta}(\\theta)\\bigr)\n-Cb_\\theta.\n\\tag{6.10}\n\\]</p><p>If the candidate set is empty, \\(b_\\theta=1\\), so boundedness suffices.</p><p>For a singleton candidate, two good centers at a point–template base have the same state, hence equal bits and the required match. Thus\n\\[\nd_{P\\theta}\\geq1-\\lambda-O(b_\\theta),\n\\qquad d_Q\\geq-1/2.\n\\]\nTheir sum is at least \\(1/2-\\lambda-O(b_\\theta)\\), which suffices.</p><p>If candidates share a row or column and all lie there, transfer puts every good point in that same row or column. Every good edge then has bit one in the row case, or zero in the column case. A bit difference requires a bad slot:\n\\[\nV_Q+V_{P\\theta}\\leq4b_\\theta.\n\\]\nThe score lower bound by minus variation proves (6.10).</p><p>If a third candidate lies outside that row or column, the witness intersection is empty or a singleton. In the empty case all edges are bad. Otherwise every good point has that one state. Two good points at a center base have equal bits and the required match, so\n\\[\nd_Q\\geq1-\\lambda-O(b_\\theta),\n\\qquad d_{P\\theta}\\geq-1/2.\n\\]</p><p>If distinct candidates share no coordinate, three give an empty intersection. With exactly two, good points occupy the two opposite corners in (6.5). At either base type, the bit determines the other endpoint state. Equal bits therefore force the required match for good pairs. Equation (6.7) gives each score at least \\(\\lambda/2-O(b_\\theta)\\). This proves (6.10).</p><p>Multiply by \\(\\pi_\\theta\\), sum, and integrate. The error is \\(o(S)\\) by (6.6), without inverse slice weights or factors counting templates. Thus the common contribution to \\(D_Q+D_{P\\theta}\\) is at least\n\\[\n\\frac{\\lambda}{4}(C_Q+C_{P\\theta})-o(S).\n\\]\nProposition 4’s rare lower bounds cancel their main terms. Its upper bounds give\n\\[\n\\frac{\\lambda}{4}(C_Q+C_{P\\theta})-o(S)\n\\leq D_Q+D_{P\\theta}\n\\leq10\\delta^2\\lambda(C_Q+C_{P\\theta})+o(S).\n\\]\nSince \\(10\\delta^2&lt;1/4\\), (6.8) follows.</p><p>Finally the common contribution to \\(D_Q\\) is at least \\(-C_Q\\). Hence\n\\[\n2\\mathbb E_\\kappa H_q^*\n-2\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*\n-C_Q-o(S)\n\\leq D_Q\n\\leq10\\delta^2\\lambda C_Q+o(S).\n\\]\nUse \\(C_Q=o(S)\\) to obtain (6.9).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, “Sampling strict crosses.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\nu\\) be a probability measure on a finite product set, and let \\(X_1,\\ldots,X_k\\) be independent samples.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\nu\\) be a probability measure on a finite product set, and let \\(X_1,\\ldots,X_k\\) be independent samples. For \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1\\), \\(k\\alpha\\geq8\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\bigl(\n\\exists y:\\nu(R_y)&lt;1-\\alpha,\\ \nX_1,\\ldots,X_k\\in R_y\n\\bigr)\n\\leq2(2k+1)^2\\,2^{-k\\alpha/2}.\n\\]</p><p>Consequently, for the templates and mixture laws in Proposition 5, eventually, uniformly in \\(q\\) and the tested level,\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\left(\n\\exists y\\in\\mathcal Y_\\theta:\n\\widetilde\\nu_q(R_y)&lt;1-k^{-1/4}\n\\ \\middle|\\ q\n\\right)\n\\leq4(2k+1)^2\\,2^{-k^{3/4}/2}.\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Adjoin independent samples \\(X_{k+1},\\ldots,X_{2k}\\). If the first half admits a cross in the event, select one by fixed ordering. For that cross, the number of misses in the new half has mean \\(k\\beta\\), with\n\\[\n\\beta=\\nu(R_y^c)&gt;\\alpha,\n\\]\nand variance at most \\(k\\beta\\). The probability of fewer than \\(k\\alpha/2\\) misses is at most\n\\[\n4/(k\\beta)\\leq1/2.\n\\]\nIt suffices to bound twice the probability that some cross contains the first half and misses at least \\(k\\alpha/2\\) of the second half.</p><p>Condition on the pooled indexed samples and split uniformly into halves. A cross induces at most \\((2k+1)^2\\) subsets: each coordinate agrees with one observed coordinate or none. If a pattern has \\(m\\) misses, the probability that all lie in the second half is zero for \\(m&gt;k\\), and otherwise at most\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k-1)\\cdots(k-m+1)}\n{(2k)(2k-1)\\cdots(2k-m+1)}\n\\leq2^{-m}.\n\\]\nA union bound proves the first assertion, also for repeated states.</p><p>For the consequence, after sampling histories independently given \\(q\\), independently draw a state from every \\(\\nu_{h_j}\\). Before revealing histories, these states are independent with law \\(\\widetilde\\nu_q\\). If a violating candidate exists, select one from the template. Conditional on it, all synthetic states lie in its cross with probability at least\n\\[\n1-k\\cdot k^{-2}=1-1/k.\n\\]\nUse the first assertion with \\(\\alpha=k^{-1/4}\\) and divide by \\(1-1/k\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2, “Transfer for restricted supports.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix a level and the edge–template law \\( \\mathbb P(p,q,\\theta)=\\mathbb P_E(p,q)\\mathbb P(\\theta\\mid q). \\) Let \\(0<\\eta_0<1/2\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a level and the edge–template law\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(p,q,\\theta)=\\mathbb P_E(p,q)\\mathbb P(\\theta\\mid q).\n\\]\nLet \\(0&lt;\\eta_0&lt;1/2\\). As a function of \\((q,\\theta)\\), select \\(U(q,\\theta)\\) that is a row, a column, or a set of at most two states. On an event \\(G\\) determined by \\((q,\\theta)\\), suppose\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\nu_q(U(q,\\theta))\\geq1-3\\eta_0^2.\n\\]\nThen the actual point state \\(X(p)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\bigl(G,\\ X(p)\\notin U(q,\\theta)\\bigr)\\leq8\\eta_0.\n\\]\nThe support type and its coordinates may depend on both \\(q\\) and \\(\\theta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Conditional on \\((q,\\theta)\\), the actual point’s history has its ordinary conditional law given \\(q\\), because point and template are independent given \\(q\\). Thus, on \\(G\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[\\nu_{H(p)}(U(q,\\theta)^c)\\mid q,\\theta]\n\\leq3\\eta_0^2.\n\\]\nThe probability that the selected support has \\(\\nu_{H(p)}\\)-mass below \\(1-\\eta_0\\) is at most \\(3\\eta_0\\).</p><p>For each history \\(h\\), define exceptional sets using only \\(\\nu_h\\). A row of mass at least \\(1-\\eta_0\\), if one exists, is unique; take its complement as the first exceptional set, or the empty set if no such row exists. Do the same for columns. Each has mass at most \\(\\eta_0\\).</p><p>If a set of at most two states has mass at least \\(1-\\eta_0\\), put\n\\[\nM_h=\\{x:\\nu_h(x)&gt;\\eta_0\\}\n\\]\nand take \\(M_h^c\\) as the third exceptional set. Every such high-mass set \\(U\\) contains \\(M_h\\), because omission of an atom above \\(\\eta_0\\) violates the mass bound. Choosing one such \\(U\\),\n\\[\n\\nu_h(M_h^c)\n\\leq\\nu_h(U^c)+\\nu_h(U\\setminus M_h)\n\\leq\\eta_0+2\\eta_0=3\\eta_0.\n\\]\nIf no such support exists, use the empty exceptional set.</p><p>The union has \\(\\nu_h\\)-mass at most \\(5\\eta_0\\). The actual point has marginal \\(\\mu\\), and its state conditional on history \\(h\\) has law exactly \\(\\nu_h\\). Thus this union has probability at most \\(5\\eta_0\\) under the whole edge–template law. Outside it and the nonconcentration event, the actual state belongs to the selected support. Add the bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 6.5, “Positive rare mass.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the incidence graph, coordinate levels, parameters, history, and scores of Propositions 1, 3, and 4.", "statement": "<p>Use the incidence graph, coordinate levels, parameters, history, and scores of Propositions 1, 3, and 4. In particular, \\(S\\to\\infty\\), and\n\\[\nH_p^*=\\int_{\\mathcal G}I_p\n\\]\nis the good rare point-exception length. Assume the conclusions of Proposition 5:\n\\[\nC_Q=o(S),\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_\\kappa H_q^*\n\\leq\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*+o(S).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\liminf_{t\\to\\infty}\n\\frac{\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*}{S}&gt;0.\n\\tag{6.12}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If the conclusion fails, pass to a subsequence with\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*=o(S).\n\\]\nProposition 5 gives \\(\\mathbb E_\\kappa H_q^*=o(S)\\), and Lemma 6.1 gives \\(T\\leq o(S)\\).</p><p>At a common level declare an ordinary edge bad if inconsistent or\n\\[\n\\nu_{H(p)}(R_{y_q})&lt;1-k^{-2}.\n\\]\nIts integrated probability is \\(o(S)\\), by Markov and (5.7).</p><p>Fix \\(p\\) and the level. Write\n\\[\nb_p=\\mathbb P(\\text{bad edge}\\mid p),\\quad\n\\nu=\\nu_{H(p)},\\quad \\zeta=k^{-2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\tau(\\nu)=\\|\\nu_A\\|_2^2+\\|\\nu_{B_1}\\|_2^2-1,\n\\]\nand let \\(\\mathcal Y_p\\) be the states of good neighbors. Every \\(y\\in\\mathcal Y_p\\) satisfies \\(\\nu(R_y)\\geq1-\\zeta\\), and the actual point belongs to every \\(R_y\\). Let \\(d_P(p)\\) be its common-level ordinary point-star score, using the full bit probability \\(\\rho_p\\). We claim\n\\[\nd_P(p)+\\tau(\\nu)\n\\geq\\frac{\\lambda}{4}\\,2\\rho_p(1-\\rho_p)\n-C(b_p+\\zeta).\n\\tag{6.13}\n\\]</p><p>A pair with a bad slot costs \\(O(b_p)\\).</p><p>If \\(\\mathcal Y_p\\) is empty, \\(b_p=1\\), while \\(d_P(p)\\geq-1/2\\), \\(\\tau(\\nu)\\geq-1\\).</p><p>If \\(\\mathcal Y_p=\\{(a,b)\\}\\), put \\(r=\\nu_A(a)\\), \\(s=\\nu_{B_1}(b)\\). Since \\(r+s\\geq1-\\zeta\\),\n\\[\n\\tau(\\nu)\\geq r^2+s^2-1\n\\geq\\tfrac12(1-\\zeta)^2-1\n\\geq-\\tfrac12-\\zeta.\n\\]\nTwo good neighbors have the same state and bit, giving\n\\[\nd_P(p)\\geq1-\\lambda-O(b_p).\n\\]</p><p>If two neighbor states share a row, their common cross lies in that row and has \\(\\nu\\)-mass at least \\(1-2\\zeta\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\tau(\\nu)\\geq(1-2\\zeta)^2-1\\geq-4\\zeta.\n\\]\nIf every neighbor state is in that row, the actual point also lies there; every good edge has bit one. Thus full-law variation is at most \\(2b_p\\), and \\(d_P(p)\\geq-2b_p\\). Columns give bit zero and the same bounds.</p><p>If a third state lies outside the row or column, the three crosses intersect in at most one state, with mass at least \\(1-3\\zeta\\). Eventually it is a singleton, so\n\\[\n\\tau(\\nu)\\geq2(1-3\\zeta)^2-1\\geq1-12\\zeta.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(d_P(p)\\geq-1/2\\), this suffices.</p><p>If no coordinate is shared by distinct states, three are impossible because the actual point belongs to all their crosses. With two states, the opposite corners carry mass at least \\(1-2\\zeta\\), so\n\\[\n\\tau(\\nu)\\geq\n2\\frac{(1-2\\zeta)^2}{2}-1\\geq-4\\zeta.\n\\]\nThe actual point is one corner, and its bit determines the good neighbor state. Equation (6.7) gives\n\\[\nd_P(p)\\geq\\lambda/2-O(b_p).\n\\]\nThis proves (6.13).</p><p>Average over \\(p\\) and integrate. The bad-edge cost is \\(o(S)\\), and\n\\[\n\\zeta W_c\\leq k^{-31/16}S=o(S).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n(\\text{common contribution to }D_P)+T\n\\geq\\frac{\\lambda}{4}C_P-o(S).\n\\]\nThe noncommon contribution is at least \\(-o(S)\\) by Proposition 4, because both exception means are \\(o(S)\\). The upper score budget and \\(T\\leq o(S)\\) give\n\\[\n\\frac{\\lambda}{4}C_P-o(S)\n\\leq D_P+T\n\\leq10\\delta^2\\lambda C_P+o(S).\n\\]\nThus \\(C_P=o(S)\\).</p><p>At any fixed level let \\(V_P,V_Q\\) be ordinary star variations. Label each vertex by its conditional majority bit, breaking ties deterministically. Since\n\\[\n\\min(\\rho,1-\\rho)\\leq2\\rho(1-\\rho),\n\\]\nthe incident edge bit disagrees with its vertex label with probability at most the corresponding variation. Endpoint labels disagree with probability at most \\(V_P+V_Q\\). If this is small, Proposition 1’s matching-label conclusion gives one bit label with marginal exception probability \\(O(V_P+V_Q)\\); the edge bit also differs from it with probability \\(O(V_P+V_Q)\\). If the sum is not small, use \\(f\\leq1/2\\). Uniformly,\n\\[\nf\\leq C(V_P+V_Q).\n\\]\nIntegration over common levels gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal C}f\\leq C(C_P+C_Q)=o(S).\n\\]\nOn \\(\\mathcal G\\), use \\(f\\leq h_P+h_Q+m\\); its integral is \\(o(S)\\). Damaged levels cost at most \\(W_b/2=o(S)\\). Consequently \\(S=\\int f=o(S)\\), a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.4, “The two-prediction score.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 6’s setting, at a common level let \\(\\nu_h\\) be the point-state law conditional on history \\(h\\), and let \\((\\nu_h)_A,(\\nu_h)_{B_1}\\) be its coordinate marginals.", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 6’s setting, at a common level let \\(\\nu_h\\) be the point-state law conditional on history \\(h\\), and let \\((\\nu_h)_A,(\\nu_h)_{B_1}\\) be its coordinate marginals. Define\n\\[\nT=\n\\int_{\\mathcal C}\n\\mathbb E_\\mu\n\\left[\n\\|(\\nu_{H(p)})_A\\|_2^2\n+\\|(\\nu_{H(p)})_{B_1}\\|_2^2-1\n\\right].\n\\tag{6.11}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*=o(S)\\), then \\(T\\leq o(S)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Draw a history with its marginal probability, then two independent points \\(p',p''\\) from \\(\\mu\\) conditional on it. This is one coupling across all levels, and both marginals are \\(\\mu\\). The expected value of\n\\[\nF(p',p'')\n=\n1_{\\{A(p')=A(p'')\\}}\n+1_{\\{B_1(p')=B_1(p'')\\}}-1\n\\]\nis the integrand defining \\(T\\).</p><p>For a distinct pair put\n\\[\nZ=z_{p'}-z_{p''},\\qquad W=w_{p'}-w_{p''}.\n\\]\nBoth are nonzero. At an entry with interval \\([\\ell,u]\\), (4.7) gives\n\\[\n\\ell\\leq-\\log|Z|_v,\\qquad u\\geq\\log|W|_v.\n\\]\nAn \\(A\\)-match requires\n\\[\ns\\leq-\\log|Z|_v+O_v(1),\n\\]\nand a \\(B_1\\)-match requires\n\\[\ns\\geq\\log|W|_v-O_v(1),\n\\]\nwhere constants vanish at finite places and are absolute at infinity. Therefore\n\\[\n\\int_\\ell^uF(p',p'')\\,ds\n\\leq-\\log|Z|_v-\\log|W|_v+O_v(1).\n\\]\nThe product formula makes the all-level integral at most an absolute constant.</p><p>On good rare levels, if neither point is exceptional, their dominant-coordinate labels match, so \\(F\\geq0\\). Generally,\n\\[\nF\\geq-1_{\\{p'\\text{ exceptional}\\}}\n-1_{\\{p''\\text{ exceptional}\\}}.\n\\]\nDamaged levels contribute at least \\(-W_b\\). Removing noncommon levels gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal C}F(p',p'')\n\\leq O(1)+H_{p'}^*+H_{p''}^*+W_b\n\\]\nfor distinct pairs.</p><p>On the diagonal the common integral is \\(W_c\\). Its probability is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{p\\in P}\n\\frac{\\mu(p)^2}{\\mu(H^{-1}(H(p)))}\n\\leq |H(P)|\\max_p\\mu(p)=t^{-1+o(1)}.\n\\]\nSince \\(W_c\\leq k^{1/16}S\\) and \\(k=t^{o(1)}\\), its contribution is \\(o(S)\\). Average the distinct-pair bound, use both marginals \\(\\mu\\), \\(W_b=o(S)\\), and \\(S\\to\\infty\\):\n\\[\nT\\leq2\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*+o(S)=o(S).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, “Tail control.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the incidence graph, levels, parameters, dominant-coordinate exceptions, and integrated edge inconsistencies from Propositions 1, 3, and 4.", "statement": "<p>Use the incidence graph, levels, parameters, dominant-coordinate exceptions, and integrated edge inconsistencies from Propositions 1, 3, and 4. Thus\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*+\\mathbb E_\\kappa H_q^*=O(S),\n\\quad\n\\mathbb E_E m_{pq}=O(1),\n\\quad\nW_c\\leq S/\\epsilon,\\quad W_b=o(S),\n\\]\nwhere \\(S\\to\\infty\\), \\(\\epsilon=k^{-1/16}\\), and \\(d_{P,\\min}\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\nR_0=\\epsilon^{-2}=k^{1/8}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu\\bigl[H_p^*1_{\\{H_p^*&gt;R_0S\\}}\\bigr]=o(S).\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(C_0\\) so that each exception mean is at most \\(C_0S\\) eventually. Put\n\\[\nT_0=\\{p:H_p^*&gt;R_0S\\},\\qquad\nA_t=\\mathbb E_\\mu[H_p^*1_{T_0}].\n\\]\nOn \\(T_0\\), the deterministic terms in Lemma 7.1 satisfy\n\\[\n\\frac{W_c+W_b+2\\log2}{R_0S}\n\\leq\\epsilon+\\frac{W_b+2\\log2}{R_0S}=o(1).\n\\]\nAbsorb them into one \\(H_p^*\\). Cap every neighbor height at \\(H_p^*\\):\n\\[\nH_p^*\\leq\n3\\bigl(\n\\min(H_p^*,H_q^*)\n+\\min(H_p^*,H_{q'}^*)\n+m_{pq}+m_{pq'}\n\\bigr)\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nfor \\(p\\in T_0\\), \\(q\\ne q'\\). If a cap changes a term, that term already equals \\(H_p^*\\) and suffices; otherwise nothing changes.</p><p>Sample \\(p\\sim\\mu\\) and two independent uniform neighbors. Restrict the left side to distinct neighbors, whose conditional probability is \\(1-1/d(p)\\), and drop that restriction on the nonnegative right:\n\\[\n(1-d_{P,\\min}^{-1})A_t\n\\leq6\\mathbb E_E[\\min(H_p^*,H_q^*)1_{T_0}]+C_m,\n\\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_m\\) bounds \\(6\\mathbb E_Em_{pq}\\).</p><p>For \\(u\\geq0\\), let\n\\[\nF_u=\\{p:H_p^*&gt;\\max(u,R_0S)\\},\\qquad\nG_u=\\{q:H_q^*&gt;u\\},\n\\]\nand \\(h_P(u)=\\mu(F_u)\\), \\(h_Q(u)=\\kappa(G_u)\\). The expansion estimate gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_E(F,G)\n\\leq\\sqrt{\\mu(F)\\kappa(G)}\n\\left[\n\\gamma^{-(1+2\\eta)}(\\mu(F)\\kappa(G))^\\eta\n+\\gamma^{-1}\\xi_t\n\\right].\n\\]\nSince \\(h_P(u)\\leq C_0/R_0\\), \\(h_Q(u)\\leq1\\), the uniform multiplier\n\\[\n\\vartheta_t\n=\\gamma^{-(1+2\\eta)}(C_0/R_0)^\\eta\n+\\gamma^{-1}\\xi_t=o(1)\n\\]\nworks at every threshold.</p><p>Layer cake, Tonelli, and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_E[\\min(H_p^*,H_q^*)1_{T_0}]\n&amp;=\\int_0^\\infty\\mathbb P_E(F_u,G_u)\\,du\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\\vartheta_t\n\\left(\\int_0^\\infty h_P(u)\\,du\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\int_0^\\infty h_Q(u)\\,du\\right)^{1/2}\\\\\n&amp;=\\vartheta_t\\sqrt{A_t\\,\\mathbb E_\\kappa H_q^*}\n\\leq\\vartheta_t\\sqrt{C_0A_tS}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe first integral is exactly \\(A_t\\), including the part below \\(R_0S\\).</p><p>Eventually (7.6) and Young’s inequality imply\n\\[\n\\frac12A_t\n\\leq6\\vartheta_t\\sqrt{C_0A_tS}+C_m\n\\leq\\frac14A_t+36C_0\\vartheta_t^2S+C_m.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\frac{A_t}{S}\n\\leq144C_0\\vartheta_t^2+\\frac{4C_m}{S}\\to0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1, “Pointwise rare-level estimate.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 7’s setting, for every \\(p\\in P\\) and every pair of distinct graph neighbors \\(q,q'\\), \\( 2H_p^* \\leq 3(H_q^*+H_{q'}^*+m_{pq}+m_{pq'}) +W_c+W_b+2\\log2. \\)", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 7’s setting, for every \\(p\\in P\\) and every pair of distinct graph neighbors \\(q,q'\\),\n\\[\n2H_p^*\n\\leq\n3(H_q^*+H_{q'}^*+m_{pq}+m_{pq'})\n+W_c+W_b+2\\log2.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At a good rare level let \\(X,U,V\\) be the exception indicators of \\(p,q,q'\\), and \\(I,I'\\) the edge-inconsistency indicators. Let \\(P_+\\) be the rare positive charge in the point-star score, and put\n\\[\nN=|Y_{pq}-Y_{pq'}|.\n\\]\nIf \\(U=V=I=I'=0\\), both bits are the same function of \\(X\\): majority bits if \\(X=0\\), minority bits if \\(X=1\\). In the latter case, the nonshared coordinate of both neighbors has the dominant label, so \\(P_+=2\\). Therefore\n\\[\nP_+\\geq2X-2(U+V+I+I'),\\qquad\nN\\leq U+V+I+I',\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nP_+-N\\geq2X-3(U+V+I+I').\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]</p><p>For a pointwise upper bound, put\n\\[\nd=z_p-z_q,\\qquad d'=z_p-z_{q'}.\n\\]\nThese are distinct and nonzero. At place \\(v\\), sort thresholds as \\(b\\leq b'\\), and let\n\\[\nu=-\\log|d-d'|_v,\\qquad \\alpha=u-b,\n\\]\n\\[\nT_v=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\alpha+\\log2,&amp;v\\text{ archimedean},\\\\\n\\alpha,&amp;v\\text{ nonarchimedean}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe triangle inequality gives \\(T_v\\geq0\\). Matching-coordinate bounds put positive charges with both bits one in \\([b-T_v,b]\\), and those with both bits zero in \\([b',b'+T_v]\\).</p><p>Only one interval can contain a rare positive. Every minority-zero level precedes every minority-one level. A zero-bit positive requires a threshold at least \\(b'\\), whereas a later one-bit positive would require a threshold below \\(b\\), which is impossible. Thus the total rare positive charge is at most \\(2T_v\\). The all-level negative charge is \\(b'-b\\).</p><p>Since\n\\[\n-\\log|(d-d')(1/d-1/d')|_v\n=2\\alpha-(b'-b),\n\\]\nthe product formula bounds rare positives minus all-level negatives by \\(2\\log2\\). Integrating (7.3) bounds the same score below. Outside good rare levels, its negative charge costs at most \\(W_c+W_b\\). Comparison gives (7.2).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 7.6, “Organized pair graph.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the incidence pieces and coordinate levels of Propositions 1, 3, and 4.", "statement": "<p>Use the incidence pieces and coordinate levels of Propositions 1, 3, and 4. Assume the conclusions of Propositions 5–7:\n\\[\nC_Q=o(S),\\qquad\n\\liminf\\frac{\\mathbb E_\\mu H_p^*}{S}&gt;0,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_\\mu[H_p^*1_{\\{H_p^*&gt;R_0S\\}}]=o(S),\n\\]\nwhere \\(R_0=k^{1/8}\\), \\(S\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>There are scales \\(J\\to\\infty\\), a sequence \\(\\omega_t\\to0\\), and a simple bipartite graph \\(\\mathcal E\\) between two copies of \\(P\\), each of size at most \\(t^{1+o(1)}\\), with at least \\(t^{2-o(1)}\\) edges, such that:</p><p class=\"li\">Each edge \\((p,r)\\) has \\(p\\ne r\\) and an assigned center \\(q(p,r)\\in Q\\) with \\(pq,rq\\in E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n\\lambda_{pr}=\n\\frac{z_p-z_{q(p,r)}}{z_r-z_{q(p,r)}}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">No edge has both endpoints on a real unit circle containing at least \\(t^{9/10}\\) points of \\(P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Uniformly over edges,\n\\[\n(z_p-z_r)(w_p-w_r)\n=2-\\lambda_{pr}-\\lambda_{pr}^{-1},\n\\tag{7.13}\n\\]\n\\[\nh(\\lambda_{pr})\\geq J/3.\n\\tag{7.14}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Uniformly over complete rectangles in \\(\\mathcal E\\),\n\\[\nh\\left(\n\\frac{\\lambda_{pr}\\lambda_{p'r'}}\n{\\lambda_{pr'}\\lambda_{p'r}}\n\\right)\n\\leq\\omega_tJ.\n\\tag{7.15}\n\\]</p><p>The four assigned centers in a rectangle need not coincide. These edge ratios are unrelated to the fixed score coefficient \\(\\lambda=\\delta^2/2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take Lemma 8.2’s band and remove Lemma 8.3’s excluded wedges. The probability remains at least\n\\[\nc/(R_0\\log(2R_0))=t^{-o(1)},\n\\]\nand all endpoints are distinct.</p><p>For an off-diagonal pair \\((p,r)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(p,r)\n=\n\\sum_{q:\\,pq,rq\\in E}\\frac1{|E|d(q)}\n\\leq\\frac2{|E|\\min_qd(q)}.\n\\]\nThere are at most two possible unit-circle centers. Thus retained wedges contain at least\n\\[\n\\frac{c|E|\\min_qd(q)}{2R_0\\log(2R_0)}\n\\geq t^{2-o(1)}\n\\]\ndistinct ordered endpoint pairs. They define \\(\\mathcal E\\). Select one surviving witness center for each pair.</p><p>For its jumps\n\\[\nd=z_p-z_q,\\qquad d'=z_r-z_q,\n\\]\nthe unit equations give\n\\[\n(z_p-z_r)(w_p-w_r)\n=(d-d')(1/d-1/d')\n=2-d/d'-d'/d,\n\\]\nwhich is (7.13).</p><p>The product formula and Lemma 8.1 give\n\\[\n2h(\\lambda_{pr})\n=\n\\sum_v^*|\\log|\\lambda_{pr}|_v|\n=\\|n_{pq}-n_{rq}\\|_1\n\\geq(1-\\delta_t)J.\n\\]\nEventually this implies (7.14).</p><p>Define\n\\[\nb_{pr}(v)=\\log|\\lambda_{pr}|_v+a_p(v)-a_r(v).\n\\]\nBy (7.8) and (7.11),\n\\[\n\\|b_{pr}\\|_1\\leq2\\delta_tJ\n\\]\nuniformly. On a complete rectangle the unary profiles cancel:\n\\[\n\\log\\left|\n\\frac{\\lambda_{pr}\\lambda_{p'r'}}\n{\\lambda_{pr'}\\lambda_{p'r}}\n\\right|_v\n=\nb_{pr}(v)+b_{p'r'}(v)-b_{pr'}(v)-b_{p'r}(v).\n\\]\nThe weighted absolute sum is at most \\(8\\delta_tJ\\); its height is at most \\(4\\delta_tJ\\). Take \\(\\omega_t=4\\delta_t\\). Popular-circle exclusion holds by construction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.3, “Wedge profiles.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use Proposition 8’s incidence and level setting.", "statement": "<p>Use Proposition 8’s incidence and level setting. On good rare levels put \\(X_x=I_x\\), and let \\(\\sigma(v,s)=1\\) when one is the minority bit and \\(-1\\) when zero is the minority bit. Define the point profile\n\\[\na_p(v)=\n\\int_{\\{s:(v,s)\\in\\mathcal G\\}}\n\\sigma(v,s)X_p(v,s)\\,ds.\n\\tag{7.7}\n\\]\nFor a place profile \\(b\\), write\n\\[\n\\|b\\|_1=\\sum_v^*|b(v)|.\n\\]</p><p>For an ordinary wedge \\((p,q,r)\\), there are nonnegative random variables \\(E_1,E_2\\), with\n\\[\n\\mathbb EE_1+\\mathbb EE_2=o(S),\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\|(n_{pq}-n_{rq})-(a_p-a_r)\\|_1\\leq E_1,\n\\tag{7.8}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|n_{pq}-n_{rq}\\|_1\\geq H_p^*+H_r^*-E_2.\n\\tag{7.9}\n\\]\nHere \\(n_{pq}(v)=-\\log|z_p-z_q|_v\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(I_{pq}\\) denote the levelwise inconsistency indicator. On a good rare level, write \\(b\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) for the majority bit. Consistency gives\n\\[\nY_{pq}=b+\\sigma(X_p+X_q)\n\\]\nunless both endpoints are exceptional or the edge is inconsistent. The absolute residual is always at most\n\\[\n2(X_pX_q+I_{pq}).\n\\]\nSubtract the two edge identities; the center contribution cancels. At each place,\n\\[\nn_{pq}(v)-n_{rq}(v)\n=\\int(Y_{pq}-Y_{rq})\\,ds.\n\\]\nThus (7.8) holds with\n\\[\nE_1=\n\\int_{\\mathcal C}|Y_{pq}-Y_{rq}|\n+W_b\n+2\\int_{\\mathcal G}\n(X_pX_q+X_rX_q+I_{pq}+I_{rq}).\n\\]\nIts mean is \\(o(S)\\): the common term averages to \\(C_Q=o(S)\\), joint edge exceptions integrate to \\(o(S)\\), and the remaining terms are controlled by Proposition 4.</p><p>There is also the exact identity\n\\[\n\\|n_{pq}-n_{rq}\\|_1=\\int|Y_{pq}-Y_{rq}|.\n\\]\nAt every good rare level,\n\\[\n|Y_{pq}-Y_{rq}|\n\\geq X_p+X_r\n-2(X_pX_r+X_pX_q+X_rX_q+I_{pq}+I_{rq}).\n\\]\nIf no subtracted term is present, exactly one exceptional point, a normal center, and consistent edges give different bits. If any subtracted term is present, the right side is nonpositive. Set\n\\[\nE_2=\n2\\int_{\\mathcal G}\n(X_pX_r+X_pX_q+X_rX_q+I_{pq}+I_{rq}).\n\\]\nProposition 4’s joint-exception and inconsistency bounds give \\(\\mathbb EE_2=o(S)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.4, “A band of accurate wedges.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 8’s setting, with \\(E_1,E_2\\) from Lemma 8.1, there are a constant \\(c>0\\), a sequence \\(\\delta_t\\to0\\), and \\(J\\to\\infty\\), such that a set of ordinary wedges of probability at least …", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 8’s setting, with \\(E_1,E_2\\) from Lemma 8.1, there are a constant \\(c&gt;0\\), a sequence \\(\\delta_t\\to0\\), and \\(J\\to\\infty\\), such that a set of ordinary wedges of probability at least\n\\[\n\\frac{c}{R_0\\log(2R_0)}=t^{-o(1)}\n\\tag{7.10}\n\\]\nsatisfies, uniformly,\n\\[\nJ\\leq H_p^*+H_r^*\\leq2J,\\qquad\nE_1+E_2\\leq\\delta_t(H_p^*+H_r^*).\n\\tag{7.11}\n\\]\nEvery such wedge has \\(p\\ne r\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nW=H_p^*+H_r^*,\\qquad Z=E_1+E_2.\n\\]\nProposition 6 gives a fixed \\(c_0&gt;0\\) with \\(\\mathbb EW\\geq4c_0S\\) eventually. Choose \\(\\delta_t\\to0\\) slowly enough that \\(\\mathbb EZ/\\delta_t=o(S)\\).</p><p>Before choosing a band, discard \\(Z&gt;\\delta_tW\\). The lost weight is at most\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[W1_{\\{Z&gt;\\delta_tW\\}}]\n\\leq\\mathbb EZ/\\delta_t=o(S).\n\\]\nDiscard \\(W&lt;c_0S\\), losing at most \\(c_0S\\). If \\(W&gt;2R_0S\\), its larger summand exceeds \\(R_0S\\) and is at least \\(W/2\\). Proposition 7 gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[W1_{\\{W&gt;2R_0S\\}}]\n\\leq\n2\\mathbb E[H_p^*1_{\\{H_p^*&gt;R_0S\\}}]\n+\n2\\mathbb E[H_r^*1_{\\{H_r^*&gt;R_0S\\}}]\n=o(S).\n\\]\nThe surviving weighted mass is at least \\(2c_0S\\), and \\(W\\leq2R_0S\\) there, so its probability is at least \\(c_0/R_0\\).</p><p>Partition \\([c_0S,2R_0S]\\) into \\(O(\\log(2R_0))\\) dyadic bands, with half-open endpoints except at the end. One has the asserted probability. Its lower endpoint \\(J\\) satisfies \\(J\\geq c_0S\\to\\infty\\). Since \\(k=t^{o(1)}\\), the probability is \\(t^{-o(1)}\\).</p><p>If \\(p=r\\), the left side of (7.9) is zero, but the right side is at least \\((1-\\delta_t)W&gt;0\\) eventually. Such a wedge cannot survive.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.5, “Popular-circle deletion.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Call a real unit circle popular if it contains at least \\(t^{9/10}\\) points of \\(P\\).", "statement": "<p>Call a real unit circle popular if it contains at least \\(t^{9/10}\\) points of \\(P\\). Under the original ordinary wedge law,\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(p,r\\text{ lie on a common popular circle})\n\\leq\nt^{-3/5+o(1)}+t^{-9/5+o(1)}\n+\\frac2{\\min_qd(q)}.\n\\tag{7.12}\n\\]\nThis is\n\\[\no\\bigl(1/(R_0\\log(2R_0))\\bigr).\n\\]\nDeleting these wedges from the set in Lemma 8.2 preserves (7.10), after decreasing its constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(M_0\\) be the number of popular circles. Two distinct real points lie on at most two unit circles, so\n\\[\nM_0\\binom{\\lceil t^{9/10}\\rceil}{2}\n\\leq2\\binom{|P|}{2},\n\\qquad M_0\\leq t^{1/5+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe set \\(P_{\\mathrm{mult}}\\) of points on at least two popular circles has size at most\n\\[\n2\\binom{M_0}{2}\\leq t^{2/5+o(1)}.\n\\]\nProposition 1’s degree bounds give\n\\[\n\\max_p\\mu(p)\\leq t^{-1+o(1)},\\qquad\n\\max_q\\kappa(q)\\leq t^{-2+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThus wedges hitting \\(P_{\\mathrm{mult}}\\) at either endpoint have probability at most \\(t^{-3/5+o(1)}\\). Centers in \\(Q\\) whose own unit circle is popular number at most \\(M_0\\), costing at most \\(t^{-9/5+o(1)}\\).</p><p>For remaining wedges, fix \\(p\\notin P_{\\mathrm{mult}}\\) and a center \\(q\\) whose own circle is not popular. The point belongs to at most one popular circle. That circle differs from the unit circle centered at \\(q\\); their intersection has at most two points. Since \\(r\\) is an original uniform neighbor of \\(q\\), the conditional probability it shares a popular circle with \\(p\\) is at most \\(2/d(q)\\). This proves (7.12).</p><p>The first two terms are negligible relative to (7.10), since \\(R_0=k^{1/8}\\), \\(k=t^{o(1)}\\). For the third use the explicit parameter condition \\(k^{20}/\\min_qd(q)\\to0\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{R_0\\log(2R_0)}{\\min_qd(q)}\n=\n\\frac{k^{1/8}\\log(2k^{1/8})}{k^{20}}\n\\frac{k^{20}}{\\min_qd(q)}\n\\to0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 8.1, “Obstruction to multiplicative rectangles.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is no sequence with the following properties.", "statement": "<p>There is no sequence with the following properties.</p><p>At index \\(n\\), let \\(t_n\\to\\infty\\), \\(J_n\\to\\infty\\), and let \\(P_n\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) be a finite set of algebraic points with\n\\[\n|P_n|\\leq t_n^{1+o(1)}.\n\\]\nLet \\(G_n\\subset P_n\\times P_n\\), using two copies of \\(P_n\\), have at least \\(t_n^{2-o(1)}\\) edges. Assign every edge a nonzero algebraic number \\(\\lambda_{pr}\\). For \\(p=(x_p,y_p)\\), put\n\\[\nz_p=x_p+iy_p,\\qquad w_p=x_p-iy_p.\n\\]\nAssume, for an absolute \\(c&gt;0\\), uniformly over edges and complete rectangles respectively,\n\\[\n(z_p-z_r)(w_p-w_r)\n=2-\\lambda_{pr}-\\lambda_{pr}^{-1},\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\n\\[\nh(\\lambda_{pr})\\geq cJ_n,\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\n\\[\nh\\left(\n\\frac{\\lambda_{pr}\\lambda_{p'r'}}\n{\\lambda_{pr'}\\lambda_{p'r}}\n\\right)=o(J_n).\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]\nAssume also that no edge has both endpoints on a real unit circle containing at least \\(t_n^{9/10}\\) points of \\(P_n\\). Here \\(h\\) is the absolute height of Proposition 3.</p><p>For the contradiction setting used in the lemmas below, choose finite normal number fields \\(F_n\\) containing all coordinates, ratios, and \\(i\\). Fix a nonprincipal ultrafilter \\(\\mathfrak u\\) on \\(\\mathbb N\\), and define\n\\[\nF=\\prod_{n\\to\\mathfrak u}F_n,\\qquad\n\\mathcal U=\\prod_{n\\to\\mathfrak u}\\mathbb C.\n\\]\nA set of indices in \\(\\mathfrak u\\) is called large; sequences agreeing on a large set are identified. Both are characteristic-zero fields, \\(F\\subset\\mathcal U\\), and \\(\\mathcal U\\) is algebraically closed. Complex conjugation acts coordinatewise.</p><p>All limits in this proposition’s proof are along \\(\\mathfrak u\\). A graph count is called full if it is at least \\(t_n^{2-o(1)}\\). Define\n\\[\nk_0=\\{[u_n]\\in F:h(u_n)=o(J_n)\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume the stated sequence exists. Heights are unchanged by the finite normal fields \\(F_n\\). A nonprincipal ultrafilter is obtained by extending the cofinite filter to a maximal proper filter. The ultraproducts are fields because inverses are defined coordinatewise on large nonzero sets. The field \\(\\mathcal U\\) is algebraically closed: a fixed positive-degree polynomial has that degree on a large set, and coordinatewise choices of complex roots give a root.</p><p>Apply Lemmas 9.1–9.4. We obtain a \\(20\\)-by-\\(5\\) generic grid, a \\(k_0\\)-derivation nonzero on every edge ratio, and a surviving left point \\(p=p_i\\) generic over \\(K_0\\). Put\n\\[\nM=K_0(V_1)=K_0(p),\\qquad\nR_\\ell=R_{i\\ell},\\qquad\nH=H_i,\\qquad A=A_i.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nR_\\ell^2=D_\\ell=L_\\ell(L_\\ell-4),\n\\]\nand Lemma 9.5 gives independence of their square classes in \\(M\\).</p><p>By the quadratic case of Kummer theory [20, Theorem 5.30 and Remark 5.32],\n\\[\n[M(R_1,\\ldots,R_j):M]=2^j,\n\\]\nand every choice of radical signs is induced by an automorphism fixing \\(M\\). This applies because \\(M\\) has characteristic zero and contains both square roots of unity. Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal M=M(R_1,\\ldots,R_j)\\subset\\mathcal U\n\\]\nusing the actual roots.</p><p>The differentiated equations are\n\\[\n2(p-r_\\ell)\\cdot H-R_\\ell A\n=\n2(p-r_\\ell)\\cdot U_\\ell-R_\\ell A'_\\ell,\n\\qquad 1\\leq\\ell\\leq j.\n\\tag{8.16}\n\\]\nAll right positions, velocities, and scalars lie in \\(K_0\\).</p><p>First suppose the three rows\n\\[\np-r_1,\\quad p-r_2,\\quad p-r_3\n\\]\nhave rank two. The determinant of the first three equations in unknowns \\(H_x,H_y,A\\) is a linear combination of \\(R_1,R_2,R_3\\) over \\(M\\). At least one coefficient is nonzero, being, up to nonzero numerical factors and signs, a two-by-two minor of the position rows. Independent sign changes imply that distinct single radicals are linearly independent over \\(M\\). Thus the determinant is nonzero.</p><p>Cramer’s rule places the existing solution in\n\\[\nH_x,H_y,A\\in M(R_1,R_2,R_3).\n\\]\nThe automorphism changing only \\(R_4\\)’s sign fixes these unknowns. Apply it to the fourth equation in (8.16) and subtract. Since \\(R_4\\ne0\\), this gives\n\\[\nA=A'_4,\n\\]\ncontradicting (8.11).</p><p>The rank condition holds except when both loci are the same line. If \\(V_2\\) is a plane or nonlinear curve, three independent generic anchors are noncollinear: a generic third point can lie on the first two’s line only if its locus is contained in that line. Three noncollinear anchors make the position rows rank two for every \\(p\\). If \\(V_2\\) is a line, the first two anchors are distinct, and rank can fail only when \\(p\\) lies on the line. Since \\(p\\) is generic on \\(V_1\\) over \\(K_0\\), that requires \\(V_1=V_2\\) to be the same line.</p><p>In this remaining case choose a nonzero line direction vector \\(v\\in K_0^2\\), and write\n\\[\np-r_\\ell=s_\\ell v,\\qquad s_\\ell\\in M^\\times.\n\\]\nOnly \\(h=v\\cdot H\\) enters (8.16). The first two equations in \\(h,A\\) have determinant\n\\[\n2(s_2R_1-s_1R_2)\\ne0\n\\]\nby independence of the single radicals. Thus\n\\[\nh,A\\in M(R_1,R_2).\n\\]\nChange only \\(R_3\\)’s sign in the third equation; subtraction gives \\(A=A'_3\\), again contradicting (8.11). The line has nonconstant projections, and in form (8.13) its direction has nonzero squared length proportional to \\(e\\ne0\\).</p><p>All sign changes take place inside the finite radical field \\(\\mathcal M\\), after Cramer’s rule locates the unknowns there. No automorphism of \\(F\\), and no compatibility of an automorphism with \\(\\partial\\), is required. The contradiction proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 9’s contradiction setting, \\(k_0\\) is a subfield of \\(F\\), and every element of \\(F\\) algebraic over \\(k_0\\) belongs to \\(k_0\\).", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 9’s contradiction setting, \\(k_0\\) is a subfield of \\(F\\), and every element of \\(F\\) algebraic over \\(k_0\\) belongs to \\(k_0\\).</p><p>Consequently, every ratio selected from graph edges is transcendental over \\(k_0\\), whereas every selected complete-rectangle defect belongs to \\(k_0^\\times\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The inequalities\n\\[\nh(uv)\\leq h(u)+h(v),\\qquad\nh(u+v)\\leq h(u)+h(v)+\\log2,\\qquad\nh(u^{-1})=h(u)\n\\]\nprove the field assertion because \\(J_n\\to\\infty\\). These statements do not depend on representatives, and \\(h(0)=0\\).</p><p>Suppose \\(u\\in F\\) satisfies a fixed-degree monic relation\n\\[\nu^m+a_{m-1}u^{m-1}+\\cdots+a_0=0,\n\\qquad a_r\\in k_0.\n\\]\nAt every finite index where the relation holds, the nonarchimedean triangle inequality gives\n\\[\n|u|_v\\leq\\max(1,\\max_r|a_r|_v).\n\\]\nAt infinity the same bound holds with factor \\(m\\): for \\(|u|_v&gt;1\\), divide by \\(|u|_v^{m-1}\\). Since archimedean weight totals one,\n\\[\nh(u)\\leq\\sum_{r=0}^{m-1}h(a_r)+\\log m=o(J_n)\n\\]\non a large set. Hence \\(u\\in k_0\\). The final assertions follow from (8.2)–(8.3).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.3, “Uniform avoidance and a generic grid,” together with the locus construction immediately preceding it", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 9’s contradiction setting, a locus at a finite index is either the entire \\((z,w)\\)-plane, of dimension two, or an irreducible plane curve over \\(\\mathbb C\\), of dimension one, with degree bounded …", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 9’s contradiction setting, a locus at a finite index is either the entire \\((z,w)\\)-plane, of dimension two, or an irreducible plane curve over \\(\\mathbb C\\), of dimension one, with degree bounded independently of the index.</p><p>Choose a pair of locus sequences retaining a full graph count and having minimal total dimension. On a large set fix their degrees and normalize one nonzero coefficient of each curve equation. Let their ultraproduct loci be\n\\[\nV_1,V_2\\subset\\mathcal U^2,\n\\qquad d_1=\\dim V_1,\\quad d_2=\\dim V_2,\n\\]\nand let \\(B_0\\subset\\mathcal U\\) be the field generated over \\(\\mathbb Q\\) by \\(i\\) and the finitely many normalized equation coefficients.</p><p>Restrict to these loci and prune to minimum degree \\(t_n^{1-o(1)}\\). Both pruned vertex sets have size \\(t_n^{1+o(1)}\\), and the edge count remains full.</p><p>Every limiting curve equation is irreducible over \\(\\mathcal U\\), has nonconstant \\(z\\)- and \\(w\\)-projections, and is invariant under\n\\[\n(z,w)\\longmapsto(\\overline w,\\overline z).\n\\]\nThe loci are not both the same unit circle.</p><p>For every fixed \\(N,j\\geq1\\), a complete \\(N\\)-by-\\(j\\) grid can be chosen in the pruned graphs on a large set so that its limiting vertices\n\\[\np_1,\\ldots,p_N\\in V_1,\\qquad\nr_1,\\ldots,r_j\\in V_2\n\\]\nhave the following property. Select both coordinates of a vertex on a plane locus and its \\(z\\)-coordinate on a curve locus. All \\(Nd_1+jd_2\\) selected coordinates are algebraically independent over \\(B_0\\); unselected curve coordinates are algebraic over the selected coordinates and \\(B_0\\). In particular,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{trdeg}_{B_0(r_1,\\ldots,r_j)}\nB_0(r_1,\\ldots,r_j,p_1,\\ldots,p_N)\n=Nd_1.\n\\tag{8.5}\n\\]\nThe selected vertices and their assigned edge ratios belong to \\(F\\).</p><p>For any intermediate field \\(B_0\\subset B\\subset\\mathcal U\\), a point of a locus \\(V\\) having transcendence degree \\(\\dim V\\) over \\(B\\) identifies its function field \\(B(V)\\) with its evaluated coordinate field. For a plane \\(B(V)=B(z,w)\\); for a curve \\(f=0\\), it is the fraction field of \\(B[z,w]/(f)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Polynomial rings \\(K[Z,W]\\) are unique factorization domains [27, Lemma 10.120.10]. Gauss’s lemma identifies factorization of primitive polynomials in \\(K[Z][W]\\) with factorization over \\(K(Z)\\). In particular, an irreducible polynomial of positive \\(W\\)-degree remains irreducible over \\(K(Z)\\); two primitive polynomials scalar-proportional over \\(K(Z)\\) differ by an element of \\(K^\\times\\).</p><p>Two distinct irreducible plane curves of degrees at most \\(D\\), over an algebraically closed field, have at most \\(D^2\\) common points by Bézout [11, Section 5.3]. Apply this over both \\(\\mathbb C\\) and \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><p>A minimum total dimension exists among \\(2,3,4\\), since two planes are available. Finite partitions fix degrees and a nonzero normalizing coefficient on a large set. The field \\(B_0\\) is countable, being generated by finitely many coefficients.</p><p>Every limiting curve equation is irreducible over \\(\\mathcal U\\). Otherwise a factorization into positive-degree factors, represented by finitely many coefficient sequences, would give a factorization at a large set of indices. The factor degrees are bounded by the fixed equation degree.</p><p>Every finite curve contains \\(t_n^{1-o(1)}\\) retained real points, by dividing the full edge count by the opposite vertex upper bound. Its image under \\((z,w)\\mapsto(\\overline w,\\overline z)\\) contains all these points. Bézout forces equality on a large set. Neither projection is constant: a curve \\(z=c\\) would have distinct symmetric image \\(w=\\overline c\\). Thus each equation has positive degree in both variables.</p><p>The loci cannot both be the same unit circle. If their normalized limiting equations were\n\\[\n(z-u)(w-v)=1,\n\\tag{8.4}\n\\]\nequality of the finitely many coefficients would hold at a large set of finite indices. Symmetry gives \\(v=\\overline u\\), again exactly on a large set. The finite loci there would be the same real unit circle, containing \\(t_n^{1-o(1)}&gt;t_n^{9/10}\\) retained vertices, contrary to the deletion hypothesis. This uses equality in the ultraproduct, not ordinary convergence of radii.</p><p>For pruning, if the initial restricted graph has \\(e_n\\) edges and \\(v_n\\) total vertices, repeatedly remove vertices of degree below \\(e_n/(2v_n)\\). Fewer than \\(e_n/2\\) edges disappear. Minimum degree is \\(t_n^{1-o(1)}\\), the count remains full, and both vertex sets have the asserted sizes.</p><p>We next establish uniform avoidance. On a plane side, for every fixed degree bound \\(D\\), some \\(b_D&gt;0\\) ensures that every nonzero polynomial of degree at most \\(D\\), even with index-dependent coefficients, vanishes on at most \\(t_n^{1-b_D}\\) pruned vertices on a large set.</p><p>If not, at each index select a polynomial maximizing its zero count on the finite vertex set; a maximum exists because only finitely many subsets occur. Failure of every fixed saving gives zero count \\(t_n^{1-o(1)}\\) along the ultrafilter. One of at most \\(D\\) irreducible factors contains \\(t_n^{1-o(1)}\\) vertices. Their minimum degrees supply a full count after restricting this side to a bounded-degree curve, contradicting minimality. On a curve side, a nonzero degree-\\(D\\) polynomial in the selected \\(z\\)-coordinate has at most \\(D\\) zeros, since real points have distinct \\(z\\)-coordinates.</p><p>Let pruned side sizes be \\(m,n'\\) and edge density \\(\\Delta\\). For independent uniform \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_N\\), let \\(X\\) be their common-neighbor fraction. Averaging opposite degrees and applying Jensen gives \\(\\mathbb EX\\geq\\Delta^N\\). Thus the number of ordered complete grids, initially allowing repetitions, is at least\n\\[\nm^N(n')^j\\mathbb EX^j\n\\geq m^N(n')^j\\Delta^{Nj}\n=t_n^{N+j-o(1)}.\n\\tag{8.6}\n\\]</p><p>For each fixed nonzero polynomial test in selected coordinates over \\(B_0\\), choose coefficient representatives. Its vanishing among all ordered vertex tuples has a power saving:\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{tuples annihilating the test}\\}\n\\leq t_n^{N+j-b}\n\\tag{8.7}\n\\]\non a large set, for some \\(b&gt;0\\). Prove this inductively by viewing the test as a polynomial in its last point block and selecting a nonzero coefficient polynomial in earlier blocks. Earlier tuples annihilating that coefficient have a saving by induction. Otherwise the last-block polynomial is nonzero and has bounded degree, so uniform plane avoidance or the curve root bound applies. Multiply by the \\(t_n^{1+o(1)}\\) upper bounds for other blocks and decrease the saving slightly. Coefficient identities and nonzero denominators hold on a large set.</p><p>Enumerate all nonzero polynomial tests over the countable field \\(B_0\\) as \\(T_1,T_2,\\ldots\\). For every fixed \\(s\\), (8.6)–(8.7) show that, on a large set, some grid avoids the first \\(s\\) tests. At index \\(n\\), choose the largest feasible prefix \\(s_n\\leq n\\) and a grid avoiding it, including representative and denominator validity in feasibility. If none is feasible, use arbitrary number-field coordinates. For every fixed \\(s\\), \\(s_n\\geq s\\) on a large set. Thus every fixed test is nonzero in the limiting grid. Only finite intersections of large sets are used.</p><p>For a curve equation \\(f(z,w)=0\\), positive \\(w\\)-degree and transcendental \\(z\\) make \\(w\\) algebraic over \\(B_0(z)\\), proving the asserted transcendence degrees. Finite selected vertices and assigned ratios lie in \\(F_n\\), so their classes lie in \\(F\\).</p><p>Finally, let \\(B_0\\subset B\\subset\\mathcal U\\) and a point on \\(V\\) have degree \\(\\dim V\\) over \\(B\\). The plane case is algebraic independence. For a curve, \\(f\\) is irreducible even over \\(\\mathcal U\\), hence over \\(B\\), and its coefficients as a polynomial in \\(w\\) have no common nonconstant factor in \\(z\\). They cannot all vanish at an abscissa in \\(\\mathcal U\\), or they would have a common linear factor. If \\(z\\) were algebraic over \\(B\\), the nonzero specialized equation would make \\(w\\) algebraic too. Thus \\(z\\) is transcendental. Gauss’s lemma makes \\(f\\) irreducible over \\(B(z)\\); every polynomial relation at the point is a multiple of \\(f\\), and every nonzero function-field denominator evaluates nonzero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.4, together with the differentiated identities (8.9)–(8.11)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Take \\(N=20\\), \\(j=5\\) in Lemma 9.2, and write \\(\\lambda_{i\\ell}\\) for the assigned grid ratios.", "statement": "<p>Take \\(N=20\\), \\(j=5\\) in Lemma 9.2, and write \\(\\lambda_{i\\ell}\\) for the assigned grid ratios. Define\n\\[\ng_i=\\lambda_{i1},\\qquad\nh_\\ell=\\frac{\\lambda_{11}}{\\lambda_{1\\ell}},\\qquad\n\\chi_{i\\ell}=\n\\frac{\\lambda_{i\\ell}\\lambda_{11}}\n{\\lambda_{i1}\\lambda_{1\\ell}}.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\chi_{i\\ell}\\in k_0^\\times\\) and\n\\[\n\\lambda_{i\\ell}=g_ih_\\ell^{-1}\\chi_{i\\ell}.\n\\tag{8.8}\n\\]</p><p>There is a \\(k_0\\)-derivation \\(\\partial:F\\to F\\) with\n\\[\n\\partial\\lambda_{i\\ell}\\ne0\n\\]\nfor every grid edge.</p><p>For such a derivation define\n\\[\nA_i=\\frac{\\partial g_i}{g_i},\\qquad\nA'_\\ell=\\frac{\\partial h_\\ell}{h_\\ell},\n\\]\n\\[\nH_i=(\\partial x_{p_i},\\partial y_{p_i}),\\qquad\nU_\\ell=(\\partial x_{r_\\ell},\\partial y_{r_\\ell}),\n\\]\n\\[\nL_\\ell(z,w)=(z-z_{r_\\ell})(w-w_{r_\\ell}),\\qquad\nR_{i\\ell}=\\lambda_{i\\ell}^{-1}-\\lambda_{i\\ell}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n2(p_i-r_\\ell)\\cdot(H_i-U_\\ell)\n=R_{i\\ell}(A_i-A'_\\ell),\n\\tag{8.9}\n\\]\n\\[\nR_{i\\ell}^2=L_\\ell(p_i)(L_\\ell(p_i)-4),\n\\tag{8.10}\n\\]\n\\[\nA_i-A'_\\ell\\ne0.\n\\tag{8.11}\n\\]\nThe dot product is the bilinear expression \\(xu+yv\\) in ordinary coordinates, without conjugation.</p>", "proof": "<p>The rectangle condition gives \\(\\chi_{i\\ell}\\in k_0^\\times\\), including repeated row or column indices where the defect equals one. The factorization is immediate. Grid vertices on each side are distinct by their algebraic independence.</p><p>Every \\(\\lambda_{i\\ell}\\) is transcendental over \\(k_0\\), by Lemma 9.1. Put one such ratio in a transcendence basis of \\(F/k_0\\), prescribe derivative one to it and zero to all other basis elements, and extend by the quotient rule. In characteristic zero, the derivation extends uniquely across the algebraic extension to \\(F\\) [7, Theorems 1.5 and 4.1]; for an infinite algebraic extension use compatible finite-subextension extensions [7, Section 5].</p><p>This gives, for each ratio, a witness derivation nonzero on it. Derivations form an \\(F\\)-vector space. A linear combination of the finitely many witnesses is nonzero on every ratio if its coefficients avoid the zeros of a product of finitely many nonzero linear forms. That product is a nonzero polynomial over the infinite field \\(F\\), so such coefficients exist.</p><p>The derivation kills \\(i\\), algebraic over \\(\\mathbb Q\\subset k_0\\), and kills all \\(\\chi_{i\\ell}\\). Differentiate (8.1) and (8.8) to obtain (8.9). Squaring \\(\\lambda^{-1}-\\lambda\\) gives (8.10). Finally\n\\[\n\\partial\\lambda_{i\\ell}/\\lambda_{i\\ell}=A_i-A'_\\ell\n\\]\nis nonzero, proving (8.11).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "Lemma 8.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the grid, derivation, right positions, right velocities, and right logarithmic derivatives of Lemma 9.3.", "statement": "<p>Use the grid, derivation, right positions, right velocities, and right logarithmic derivatives of Lemma 9.3. Let \\(K_0\\subset\\mathcal U\\) consist of all elements algebraic over\n\\[\nB_0(r_1,\\ldots,r_j,U_1,\\ldots,U_j,A'_1,\\ldots,A'_j).\n\\]\nThis field is algebraically closed. The field \\(k_0\\) is not adjoined to \\(K_0\\).</p><p>Some \\(p=p_i\\) has transcendence degree \\(d_1\\) over \\(K_0\\), and evaluation identifies\n\\[\nM:=K_0(V_1)=K_0(p).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Roots in \\(\\mathcal U\\) of polynomials over \\(K_0\\) are still algebraic over the displayed coefficient field, so \\(K_0\\) is algebraically closed.</p><p>By Lemma 9.2, over \\(B_0(r_1,\\ldots,r_j)\\) the left positions jointly have transcendence degree \\(Nd_1\\). Right velocities and scalars consist of at most \\(3j\\) elements. Adjoining them, then algebraic closure, reduces degree by at most \\(3j\\):\n\\[\n\\operatorname{trdeg}_{K_0}\nK_0(p_1,\\ldots,p_N)\\geq Nd_1-3j.\n\\tag{8.12}\n\\]\nIf no individual point had degree \\(d_1\\), subadditivity would bound this by \\(N(d_1-1)\\), contradicting \\(N&gt;3j\\). Function-field identification follows from Lemma 9.2’s evaluation assertion.</p><p>The velocity data may depend arbitrarily on left positions; the degree count allows these correlations. No derivative of a locus equation is used, and no locus coefficient is asserted constant under \\(\\partial\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.5", "orig": "Lemma 8.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Use the surviving point \\(p=p_i\\), algebraically closed field \\(K_0\\), and function field \\( M=K_0(V_1)=K_0(p) \\) from Lemma 9.4.", "statement": "<p>Use the surviving point \\(p=p_i\\), algebraically closed field \\(K_0\\), and function field\n\\[\nM=K_0(V_1)=K_0(p)\n\\]\nfrom Lemma 9.4. Write\n\\[\nL_\\ell(z,w)=(z-z_{r_\\ell})(w-w_{r_\\ell}),\\qquad\nD_\\ell=L_\\ell(L_\\ell-4)\\in M.\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\ell\\in\\{1,\\ldots,j\\}\\), there is an integer-valued valuation\n\\[\nv_\\ell:M^\\times\\to\\mathbb Z\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\nv_\\ell(D_\\ell)=1,\\qquad\nv_\\ell(D_m)=0\\quad(m\\ne\\ell).\n\\]\nConsequently the classes of \\(D_1,\\ldots,D_j\\) are independent in\n\\[\nM^\\times/(M^\\times)^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>An odd valuation detects a nonsquare. Construct the valuations over \\(K_0\\); all required noncoincidences are first established from joint genericity over \\(B_0\\).</p><p>If \\(V_1\\) is the plane, put\n\\[\nQ_\\ell=(z-z_{r_\\ell})(w-w_{r_\\ell})-4.\n\\]\nThis polynomial is primitive in \\(w\\), since its leading coefficient \\(z-z_{r_\\ell}\\) is coprime to its constant coefficient, and it is linear over \\(K_0(z)\\). Thus it is irreducible in \\(K_0[z,w]\\). Comparing coefficients of \\(z,w\\) recovers its center, so the \\(Q_\\ell\\) are distinct. None divides any\n\\[\nL_m=(z-z_{r_m})(w-w_{r_m}).\n\\]\nThe exponent of \\(Q_\\ell\\) in a rational function gives the desired valuation.</p><p>Now suppose \\(V_1\\) is a curve and either \\(V_2\\ne V_1\\) or the \\(z\\)-projection of \\(V_1\\) has degree at least two. Let \\(f(z,w)\\) be its irreducible equation and put \\(a=z_{r_\\ell}\\). The coordinate \\(a\\) is transcendental over \\(B_0\\). Hence the leading coefficient of \\(f\\) in \\(w\\) does not vanish at \\(a\\), and \\(f(a,w)\\) has simple roots: coprimality of \\(f,f_w\\) over \\(B_0(z)\\), after clearing denominators, excludes repeated roots at a transcendental abscissa.</p><p>If \\(V_2\\ne V_1\\), the generic anchor \\(r_\\ell\\) on the plane or different curve \\(V_2\\) does not belong to \\(V_1\\). Choose any root \\(b\\) of \\(f(a,w)\\); then \\(b\\ne w_{r_\\ell}\\). If \\(V_2=V_1\\) and the projection degree is at least two, choose a different root from \\(w_{r_\\ell}\\). Thus\n\\[\ns_\\ell=(a,b)\\in V_1,\\qquad\nf_w(a,b)\\ne0,\\qquad b\\ne w_{r_\\ell}.\n\\]\nThe ordinate \\(b\\) is algebraic over \\(B_0(a)\\), hence belongs to the algebraically closed field \\(K_0\\).</p><p>For \\(m\\ne\\ell\\), joint genericity implies \\(r_m\\) remains generic on \\(V_2\\) over \\(B_0(s_\\ell)\\). Adjoining the sheet ordinate is algebraic over adjoining \\(a\\), and does not reduce the independent anchor’s degree. Neither coordinate of \\(r_m\\) equals the corresponding coordinate of \\(s_\\ell\\), because both curve projections are nonconstant. Therefore \\(L_m(s_\\ell)\\ne0\\).</p><p>Also \\(L_m(s_\\ell)\\ne4\\). Otherwise generic evaluation would force \\(V_2\\) into\n\\[\n(z-a)(w-b)=4.\n\\]\nA plane cannot lie there. If \\(V_2\\) is a curve, its equation would be proportional to this irreducible equation. Normalize the \\(zw\\)-coefficient to one; its \\(w\\)-coefficient recovers \\(-a\\) from coefficients in \\(B_0\\), contradicting transcendence of \\(a\\). These are nonvanishing statements in \\(\\mathcal U\\), unchanged by placing more entries in \\(K_0\\).</p><p>Introduce \\(T\\) and solve recursively\n\\[\nf(a+T,b+c_1T+c_2T^2+\\cdots)=0\n\\]\nin \\(K_0[[T]]\\). The coefficient of each new unknown is \\(f_w(a,b)\\ne0\\). This embeds\n\\[\nK_0(V_1)\\hookrightarrow K_0((T)):\n\\]\n\\(z\\) maps to the transcendental \\(a+T\\), and irreducibility of \\(f\\) over \\(K_0(z)\\) makes the polynomial kernel exactly \\((f)\\). Now\n\\[\nL_\\ell=T(b-w_{r_\\ell}+O(T))\n\\]\nhas order one, while \\(L_\\ell-4\\) and all \\(L_m(L_m-4)\\), \\(m\\ne\\ell\\), have nonzero constant terms. Order in \\(T\\) is the desired valuation.</p><p>It remains that both loci are the same curve with projection degree one. Symmetry gives degree one for the other projection. The equation has degree one in each variable, so it is either\n\\[\nw=ez+e',\\qquad e\\ne0,\n\\tag{8.13}\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n(z-u)(w-v)=b,\\qquad b\\ne0.\n\\tag{8.14}\n\\]\nThe constants belong to \\(B_0\\). Indeed, write the equation as\n\\[\nazw+cz+dw+e_0=0.\n\\]\nIf \\(a=0\\), both linear coefficients are nonzero. If \\(a\\ne0\\), complete the product; irreducibility makes its constant nonzero. In (8.14), \\(b\\ne1\\) by the exact common-unit-circle exclusion.</p><p>In the line case, with \\(a_\\ell=z_{r_\\ell}\\),\n\\[\nL_\\ell=e(z-a_\\ell)^2.\n\\]\nThus \\(L_\\ell-4\\) has two distinct simple zeros. Choose\n\\[\nz=a_\\ell+c,\\qquad c^2=4/e,\n\\]\nand call the corresponding point \\(s_\\ell\\). It is algebraic over \\(B_0(a_\\ell)\\). For an independent anchor \\(r_m\\), either \\(L_m(s_\\ell)=0\\) or \\(4\\) would make its abscissa algebraic over this field, contradicting genericity. Order at the chosen abscissa in \\(K_0(z)\\) gives the valuation, since \\(L_\\ell=4\\) there.</p><p>In the fractional-linear case, set\n\\[\n\\zeta=\\frac{z-u}{a_\\ell-u}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nL_\\ell=-b\\frac{(\\zeta-1)^2}{\\zeta}.\n\\tag{8.15}\n\\]\nThe equation \\(L_\\ell-4=0\\) becomes\n\\[\nb\\zeta^2+(4-2b)\\zeta+b=0.\n\\]\nIts discriminant is \\(16(1-b)\\ne0\\), and both roots are nonzero. They give simple zeros at finite points with \\(z\\ne u\\). Choose such a point \\(s_\\ell\\), algebraic over \\(B_0(a_\\ell)\\). Its squared distance to an independent anchor with abscissa \\(a_m\\) is\n\\[\n-b\\frac{(z_{s_\\ell}-a_m)^2}\n{(z_{s_\\ell}-u)(a_m-u)}.\n\\]\nThis is a nonconstant rational function in \\(a_m\\): numerator degree two, denominator degree one, and \\(z_{s_\\ell}\\ne u\\). It cannot equal zero or four at the transcendental \\(a_m\\) over \\(B_0(s_\\ell)\\). Order at the chosen abscissa gives the valuation.</p><p>These cases exhaust the possibilities. Every nonempty product \\(\\prod_{\\ell\\in I}D_\\ell\\) has valuation one at any \\(v_\\ell\\) with \\(\\ell\\in I\\), hence is not a square.</p><p>The exclusion \\(b=1\\) is essential to this construction: in that case\n\\[\nL_\\ell(L_\\ell-4)\n=\\left(\\frac{\\zeta^2-1}{\\zeta}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nAll valuations above were constructed after enlarging to \\(K_0\\); no preservation of nonsquares under coefficient-field enlargement is assumed.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 12, "cite"], [1, 16, "cite"], [1, 18, "cite"], [1, 20, "cite"], [1, 25, "cite"], [1, 28, "cite"], [1, 30, "cite"], [1, 32, "cite"], [1, 35, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [7, 1, "cite"], [8, 0, "cite"], [8, 16, "cite"], [8, 18, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 11, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [11, 8, "cite"], [12, 0, "cite"], [12, 18, "cite"], [12, 25, "cite"], [12, 28, "cite"], [12, 30, "cite"], [12, 32, "cite"], [12, 36, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [14, 16, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 12, "cite"], [17, 12, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"], [18, 25, "cite"], [18, 28, "cite"], [18, 30, "cite"], [18, 32, "cite"], [18, 33, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"], [20, 18, "cite"], [20, 24, "cite"], [21, 18, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [21, 24, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 18, "cite"], [24, 18, "cite"], [25, 0, "cite"], [25, 28, "cite"], [25, 32, "cite"], [26, 25, "cite"], [27, 25, "cite"], [28, 0, "cite"], [28, 32, "cite"], [28, 34, "cite"], [29, 28, "cite"], [30, 0, "cite"], [30, 32, "cite"], [30, 34, "cite"], [31, 30, "cite"], [32, 0, "cite"], [33, 32, "cite"], [33, 34, "cite"], [34, 32, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [35, 32, "cite"], [36, 0, "cite"], [37, 36, "cite"], [37, 39, "cite"], [38, 36, "cite"], [38, 39, "cite"], [38, 40, "cite"], [39, 36, "cite"], [39, 40, "cite"], [40, 36, "cite"], [40, 41, "cite"], [41, 36, "cite"]]});
